82":5,"383":5,"384":5,"385":5,"386":5,"387":5,"388":5,"389":5,"390":5,"391":5,"392":5,"393":5,"394":5,"395":5,"396":5,"397":5,"398":5,"399":5,"400":5,"401":5,"402":5,"403":5,"404":5,"405":5,"406":5,"407":5,"408":5,"409":5,"410":5,"411":5,"412":5,"413":5,"414":5,"415":5,"416":5,"417":5,"418":5}}},"docs":{"docs":{"1":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"page","content":"Changesets","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets"},"2":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#changesets-support","content":"Changesets support"},"3":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"Single-project repositories without a config file"},"4":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-checks-in-github-actions","content":"Single-project checks in GitHub Actions"},"5":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Packages and tags"},"6":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#pre-releases","content":"Pre-releases"},"7":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#snapshots","content":"Snapshots"},"8":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#changesets-support","content":"The changeset commands manage versioning, changelogs and release tags for single-package repositories and monorepos, including this repository. They read Changesets-style Markdown and load JavaScript configs with descriptors for npm and Composer manifests, plugin and theme headers, readme Stable tags, PHP constants, and Git tags. Use JavaScript configs with ESM (`export default`) or CommonJS (`module.exports`). Frontmatter uses `package-name: patch|minor|major` entries. For pre-releases, use the built-in pre-release workflow; the parser supports this simple frontmatter format rather than general YAML or the Changesets pre mode."},"9":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"The `changeset-*` commands can release one project at the repository root without a `wp-release.config.*` file. Run them from the root directory. With no version descriptor flags, they detect a root `package.json` or `composer.json` with a version, a plugin entry PHP file with a `Plugin Name` header, or a theme `style.css` with a `Theme Name` header. A detected plugin also updates the Stable tag in `readme.txt`, or `readme.md` when no Stable tag is found in `readme.txt`. Multiple version sources, multiple plugin entry files and npm workspaces require explicit descriptors or a config; files belonging to different projects aren't silently synchronized. A directory without a recognized version source still requires a config or explicit descriptors."},"10":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"For a root npm package, the complete workflow needs no project flags:"},"11":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"A single configured package is selected automatically for `changeset-add --bump` when `--since` isn't supplied, and for `changeset-tag --version`; `--since` still selects changed packages. npm and Composer projects use their root manifest's name as the changeset identifier when that manifest supplies the version; other projects use the root directory's basename. `--project-name <name>` pins the identifier of a repository without a manifest name, so local checkouts and CI don't need matching directory names; a config sets the same identifier with `id`. The fallback uses `.changeset/`, `CHANGELOG.md`, tags named `v{version}`, and the `origin` remote. There is no default comparison branch: pass `--since` or `--base-branch` when status checks should compare changes. Readme changelog updates are opt-in."},"12":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"Explicit descriptors replace automatic detection and define the entire version target list. For a plugin whose version appears in its header, readme and a PHP constant:"},"13":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"The same project flags belong on `changeset-add`, `changeset-status`, `changeset-version` and `changeset-tag`. Keep them consistent across all four commands, for example in command-specific npm scripts. An accompanying `package.json` used for build tools isn't changed unless it is included explicitly."},"14":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                            | Version descriptor                     |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-package-json <file>` | npm manifest version source and target |","table":"table-0"},"15":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                             | Version descriptor                                                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `--version-composer-json <file>` | Composer manifest version source and target; a target without a `version` field is skipped, while a source must have one |","table":"table-0"},"16":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                                | Version descriptor                                |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-wordpress-plugin <file>` | WordPress plugin Version header source and target |","table":"table-0"},"17":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                               | Version descriptor                               |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `--version-wordpress-theme <file>` | WordPress theme Version header source and target |","table":"table-0"},"18":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                                    | Version descriptor                                                                                        |\n| --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-wordpress-stable-tag <file>` | WordPress readme Stable tag source and target; keeps its stable version during pre-releases and snapshots |","table":"table-0"},"19":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                                       | Version descriptor                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-php-constant <file>#<constant>` | PHP `define()` or `const` version source and target |","table":"table-0"},"20":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Flag                          | Version descriptor                                                                                                      |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-git-tag <pattern>` | Current version from existing stable Git tags, with a pattern such as `v{version}`; source-only, never a version target |","table":"table-0"},"21":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"File descriptor flags are repeatable. The source is inferred from a sole descriptor, or a unique descriptor other than a Stable tag. Otherwise use `--version-from <kind>`, where kind is `package-json`, `composer-json`, `wordpress-plugin`, `wordpress-theme`, `wordpress-stable-tag`, `php-constant` or `git-tag`. For repeated kinds, qualify the selector with the exact descriptor value: `--version-from package-json:package.json` or `--version-from 'php-constant:my-plugin.php#MY_PLUGIN_VERSION'`. Argument order doesn't select the source. A Git source requires a matching existing stable tag; its pattern selects the tags to read, while `--tag-format` controls tags to create. With multiple descriptors, explicitly select `git-tag` if it is the authoritative source."},"22":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option           | Config equivalent                                                                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--project-name <name>` | `id`, the literal changeset identifier of the root package, instead of the manifest name or checkout directory basename |","table":"table-1"},"23":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option            | Config equivalent                                                 |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--base-branch <branch>` | `baseBranch`, used with the remote for the default comparison ref |","table":"table-1"},"24":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-22","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option     | Config equivalent                                       |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--remote <name>` | `remote`, used for comparisons and pushing release tags |","table":"table-1"},"25":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-23","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option            | Config equivalent                             |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `--changesets-dir <dir>` | `changesets`, relative to the repository root |","table":"table-1"},"26":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-24","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option                                  | Config equivalent                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--readme-changelog` / `--no-readme-changelog` | `readmeChangelog`, independent of updating the readme Stable tag |","table":"table-1"},"27":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-25","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option                       | Config equivalent                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--changelog-insert-after <marker>` | `changelogInsertAfter`; must exist in `CHANGELOG.md` and, with readme changelog updates, the plugin readme |","table":"table-1"},"28":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-26","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"| Shared option           | Config equivalent                               |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `--tag-format <format>` | `tagFormat`, replacing the default `v{version}` |","table":"table-1"},"29":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-27","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"An explicit `--config` must load successfully. Otherwise a discovered config takes precedence over the single-project fallback. Single-project descriptor and settings flags cannot be mixed with a config file; put those settings in the config instead. The existing global `--tag-format` override works in both modes. Config files remain the place for package layouts, dependents, custom version and changelog callbacks, deployment destinations and distribution settings. Packaging and deployment commands retain their existing config requirements."},"30":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-28","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"Typical release flow, from a feature pull request to the publish workflows triggered by release tags:"},"31":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-29","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"An empty changeset marks a feature PR as no-release. The version workflow consumes it without changing versions, and a release PR containing only its deletion produces no tags."},"32":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-30","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-repositories-without-a-config-file","content":"`version.from` selects the current version; `version.to` lists release targets. Use `phpConstantVersion( { file, constant } )` to sync a PHP constant."},"33":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-31","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-checks-in-github-actions","content":"There are no dedicated build or changeset wrapper actions. Install the CLI in your project and run it directly. A root npm package can check a pull request with:"},"34":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-32","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#single-project-checks-in-github-actions","content":"For a plugin with several version targets, put the same descriptor flags in npm scripts for all four changeset commands. Full Git history and tags are needed for comparisons, Git version sources and release notes. Keep the checkout directory name consistent when it supplies the changeset identifier. Directory artifacts use `npx --no-install wp-release build --output dist/my-plugin --github-output`; upload that directory with your artifact action. `build` writes manifest sidecars outside the directory and does not accept the ZIP-only embedding, codec or manifest-filename flags."},"35":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-33","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"},"36":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-34","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**Packages** are the directories that the `glob` of a `packages` entry matches under `root`, with `*`, `?` and `**`, like `plugins/*`, or `.` for a repository with one package at its root. The settings of an entry apply to each of its packages. Discovery never looks inside `.git`, `node_modules`, `vendor` or `dist` directories. A package is identified by its path, or with `idFrom` by the name in its `package.json` or `composer.json`, or with a literal `id` when the group matches exactly one package, and commands that take a package accept a path or identifier."},"37":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-35","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**Templates** name the tags and the deploy targets of a package with these placeholders:"},"38":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-36","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"| Placeholder | Value                                                                 | `plugins/alpha` | Root package |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- | ------------ |\n| `{path}`    | Package directory relative to `root`, empty for a package at the root | `plugins/alpha` | (empty)      |","table":"table-2"},"39":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-37","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"| Placeholder | Value              | `plugins/alpha`                                     | Root package                             |\n| ----------- | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `{name}`    | Package identifier | `plugins/alpha`, or its manifest name with `idFrom` | the manifest name, or the directory name |","table":"table-2"},"40":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-38","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"| Placeholder | Value            | `plugins/alpha` | Root package |\n| ----------- | ---------------- | --------------- | ------------ |\n| `{version}` | Released version | `1.2.0`         | `1.2.0`      |","table":"table-2"},"41":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-39","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Deploy templates also have `{branch}`, `{ref}` and `{revision}` (see Deploy configured packages), and any template can be a callback that receives the package, for names that the placeholders don't cover, like the last directory of the path."},"42":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-40","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**`tagFormat`** names the release tags, `{path}/{version}` by default, like `plugins/alpha/1.2.0`. Empty placeholders leave no leading or repeated slashes in a tag, so a package at the root gets plain version tags, like `1.2.0`, from the same default. Deploy templates, like repository URLs, are filled in as they are. A `gitTagVersion()` version source reads the tags with its own `pattern`, also `{path}/{version}` by default, so set both when you change the format."},"43":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-41","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**`releaseTitle`** titles the release, like the `title` that `changeset-tag` reports for creating a GitHub Release. The default is `name@version`, like `plugins/alpha@1.2.0`, without the prefix for a package at the repository root, which is titled `1.2.0` like its tag. Like `tagFormat`, it supports `{name}`, `{path}`, `{version}` and `{tag}` placeholders: `releaseTitle: '{version} (beta)'`, or a callback that receives `{ package, version, tag }` like the release notes callback. A template without a placeholder fails at load time, since it would name every release alike. `changeset-tag --release-title <template>` replaces the `releaseTitle` of the config for one command, like `--tag-format` replaces `tagFormat`."},"44":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-42","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**`--tag-format <format>`** replaces the `tagFormat` of the config for one command, for a workflow or job that names its tags differently, like `--tag-format '{name}@{version}'`. Every command that creates or reads the same tags needs the same format: `changeset-tag`, which creates them and finds the previous tag for the release notes, `changeset-version --prerelease`, which numbers pre-releases from them, and `deploy-git` and `deploy-svn --release-ref`, which read the package and version of a tag."},"45":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-43","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**Slugs** are a WordPress concept: the folder of a plugin or theme. Only the commands that produce something for WordPress use one: the `{slug}/` root folder and name of a `zip`, the `checksums` manifest, the WordPress.org slug of `deploy-svn`, and `version-check`. Each takes `--slug`, which defaults to the name of the working directory, or of the package directory with `zip --package`, and an SVN deploy target takes `slug`, which defaults to the name of the package directory. Tags and Git deploy targets don't have a slug."},"46":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-44","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"**`remote`** is the git remote that `changeset-tag --push` pushes tags to and that the base branch is compared on, `origin` by default."},"47":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-45","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"The default strategy bumps stable `x.y.z` versions with SemVer rules, and the `x.y` and `x.y.z.w` versions common in WordPress plugins the same way: the bumped part is raised, the parts after it reset to `0`, and the version keeps its number of parts. A minor bump of `2.1` is `2.2` and a major bump is `3.0`, while a patch bump adds the third part, `2.1.1`. A patch bump of `1.2.3.4` is `1.2.4.0`, so raising only the fourth part needs a custom strategy. Configure `nextVersion( { package, bump, currentVersion, changesets } )` for other formats; changeset bumps remain `patch`, `minor` or `major`. Pre-releases are added to the next version with `--prerelease`, described below."},"48":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-46","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Without `--since`, `changeset-status` compares against `<remote>/<baseBranch>`, where `remote` in the config defaults to `origin` and is also the remote that `changeset-tag --push` pushes tags to; fetch that ref or pass `--since` when using a fork or different upstream. `loadChangesetConfig()` discovers a conventional config at the repository root, unless `--config <path>` selects one explicitly."},"49":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-47","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Add release notes in a feature PR with `npx wp-release changeset-add`. In a terminal, it lists the packages, marks the ones that changed since `<remote>/<baseBranch>` (or `--since <ref>`) without a changeset, and asks for the packages to release, a `patch`, `minor` or `major` bump for each, and the release note. Choose packages by number, identifier or path, or press Enter to release the changed ones; `none` adds an empty changeset. Changed files include uncommitted and untracked ones, since changesets are usually added before committing."},"50":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-48","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Without a terminal, or for scripts, pass the changeset as options: `npx wp-release changeset-add --package @vendor/plugin --bump patch --summary \"Fix the checkout validation.\"`. Repeat package and bump flags for multiple packages, or use `--empty` when no release is needed. A package can also be named by its path under `root`, like `plugins/alpha`, and the changeset records its identifier; `changeset-version --package` and `changeset-tag --package` accept paths too. A single `--bump` without `--package` releases every changed package without a changeset, like `changeset-add --bump patch --summary \"…\"` to fix a failing `changeset-status` check. `--json` and `--github-output` never ask."},"51":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-49","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"In CI, `changeset-status --since origin/main` requires a changeset added by the PR; changesets already on the base do not count, while an empty PR changeset records an explicit no-release decision. Missing-change annotations identify an affected file."},"52":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-50","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"On the release branch, preview with `changeset-version --dry-run`, then run `changeset-version` to update version targets and changelogs. By default, new Markdown entries go before the first version heading; `changelogInsertAfter` sets a literal insertion marker for `CHANGELOG.md` and, with `readmeChangelog`, the plugin readme. Put the marker in both files; a missing marker is an error. Without a marker, plugin readmes use the `== Changelog ==` heading. Failed updates are rolled back for safe retries."},"53":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-51","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Configure `changelogEntry( { package, changeset, commit } )` to format the changelog entry of each changeset, where `commit` is the `{ hash, shortHash, subject }` of the commit that added the changeset file, if it's committed. The formatted entries go into `CHANGELOG.md`, the `changelog` of each release in the `changeset-version` result, and so the release notes; plugin readmes keep the plain summaries. Without the callback, entries are the plain summaries. For example, to link each entry to its commit:"},"54":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-52","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Configure `dependents` to re-release packages that bundle a package when it releases. The dependents come from what the packages declare: a package is a dependent of a source when the spec that it declares in its npm `dependencies` or `optionalDependencies` (or the sections that the policy adds) resolves the current version of the source or points at the workspace, and it re-releases when the next version of the source falls outside the declared range, or always with `when: 'always'`. A pin to an older version selects a registry release instead and `changeset-status` warns about the drift until the pin resolves the package again. Versioning a dependent rewrites its declared specs of the source to the released version, keeping their range prefixes, so an exact pin matches the released workspace again and the built bundle matches the changelog. Wildcards and unpinned ranges accept the release without one, like the `devDependencies` of a package. The derived dependents appear in `changeset-status`, `changeset-version` and `changeset-tag` like any other release, and a dependent that releases for its own reasons keeps its own bump and appends the bundle note:"},"55":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-53","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"Excluded packages still release with their own changesets and stay out of the automatic re-releases."},"56":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-54","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"When the changesets of a pull request release a source, `changeset-status` requires no changeset for a dependent whose only change is the spec of that source in its `package.json`, since versioning rewrites the spec and the bundle note announces it. Any other change to the dependent, like another manifest field or dependency, documentation or code, needs its own changeset."},"57":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-55","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"The bundle changelog entry defaults to `Bundle <source> <version>.` and is formatted with `dependents.changelog( { package, source } )`:"},"58":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-56","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"`dependents.sections` lists the npm dependency sections that declare a source, defaulting to `dependencies` and `optionalDependencies`; add `devDependencies` for dependencies that end up in the dependent's build, or `peerDependencies` to scan them."},"59":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-57","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"The `dependents.dependency` hooks extend the built-in npm scan instead of replacing it, so that both work behind the same range rule and rewrite:"},"60":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-58","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"After versioning, run `npm install --package-lock-only` so that the root lockfile resolves the rewritten pins to the workspace links again; the `Prepare Release` workflow (`prepare-release.yml` in this monorepo) does it with its release pull request."},"61":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-59","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"To release packages separately, for example with a release pull request for each, pass `--package <name>`, repeatable, to version only those packages. A changeset that also names other packages keeps only their entries and its summary, so it still applies to their releases with the same bump. Changesets without packages are left for a run without `--package`."},"62":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-60","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"The `changeset-version` result lists each release with its `package`, `bump`, `currentVersion`, `nextVersion` and `changelog` entries, a Markdown list like the one added to `CHANGELOG.md`. Use it to name and describe the release pull request in a repository script, or call `versionChangesets()` from the library and format the entries of each release with `formatChangelogEntries( release.changesets )`."},"63":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-61","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"After merging the release PR, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --push` pushes tags only for packages changed by that release. It pushes one tag at a time because GitHub doesn't trigger workflows for a push of more than three tags. wp-release doesn't create GitHub Releases: with `--json` or `--github-output`, each entry of `tags` has the `tag`, a `title` like `plugins/alpha@1.2.0` or, for a package at the repository root, the release title like `1.2.0` (see releaseTitle, and `--release-title` for overriding it in one command), the release `notes` and a `prerelease` flag, for a workflow step like `softprops/action-gh-release` to create the release (see the example below). For initial adoption, preview all missing tags with `changeset-tag --dry-run` (without `--since`), then push if needed. To release each package with a separate workflow instead, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --untagged --github-output` lists the releases that don't have a tag yet, without creating any. Tag pushes need Git write credentials."},"64":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-62","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"To release a single package, for example from a manual workflow, pass `--package <name>`, repeatable, and with one package `--version <version>` to fail unless the package is at that version. Packages versioned by git tags have no version file, so for them `--version` is the version to tag. Before a tag is created, every `version.to` target must match the version, so a plugin `Version` header that disagrees with its readme `Stable tag` blocks the tag. Versions with a pre-release part, like `1.0.0-rc.1`, have `prerelease` set, so their GitHub Releases can be marked as pre-releases. This example selects one package, so `tags[0]` is its release; these fields are nested in the JSON result:"},"65":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-63","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"The release notes are the version's `CHANGELOG.md` section, or without one the commits that changed the package since its previous tag, the highest version below the released one, skipping pre-releases for a stable version. Configure `releaseNotes( { package, version, tag, previousTag, changelog, commits, notes } )` to format them, for example to add links; `notes` is the default text and `commits` lists `{ hash, shortHash, subject }`, newest first. `changeset-tag --package <name> --dry-run` prints them without creating the tag."},"66":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-64","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#packages-and-tags","content":"For local development, run the TypeScript source with `npm run wp-release -- <command>` (for example, `npm run wp-release -- changeset-status --since origin/main`). GitHub workflows use the same CLI for versioning and tagging. `SUBTREE_DEPLOY_TOKEN` lets them update the release PR and push tags, and the `Tag Release` workflow passes it to `softprops/action-gh-release` to create the GitHub Releases."},"67":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-65","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#pre-releases","content":"`changeset-version --prerelease <id>` (the `prerelease` option of `versionChangesets()`) releases pre-releases of the next versions, like `1.3.0-rc.1` for a minor bump of `1.2.0`. The identifier, like `alpha`, `beta` or `rc`, can have a number, like `rc.2`, which is used as is and fails when the version is already tagged. Without a number, the number is one above the highest existing tag of that identifier and next stable version, read with the `tagFormat` of the package, so the first one is `rc.1`, and the numbering starts again at `.1` when later changesets raise the next version. The dependents of a source become pre-releases too, so that they bundle its pre-release, unless they're left out with `--no-dependents`."},"68":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-66","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#pre-releases","content":"A pre-release writes every version target except the readme `Stable tag`, which keeps the last stable version since WordPress.org offers it to every site, and `changeset-tag` skips the `Stable tag` when it verifies a pre-release. The changelog gets a section for the pre-release, like `## 1.3.0-rc.1 (2026-10-12)`, so that `changeset-tag` finds its release notes, and the changesets are consumed like in any release."},"69":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-67","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#pre-releases","content":"Since a pre-release consumes the changesets that the stable release needs, run it on a branch that isn't merged, and tag that branch commit:"},"70":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-68","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#pre-releases","content":"The base branch keeps its stable versions and pending changesets, so the stable release later versions them as usual, in one changelog section. The tag keeps the pre-release commit reachable after the branch is reset or deleted. The release notes of a stable release compare with the previous stable release, skipping the pre-releases before it, and the `git-tag` version source ignores pre-release tags, so packages versioned by git tags keep building on their last stable version."},"71":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets-69","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/changesets#snapshots","content":"`changeset-version --snapshot <id>` (the `snapshot` option of `versionChangesets()`) releases snapshots of the next versions for test builds, like `1.3.0-pr-251.20261012183005` for a pull request: the next version with the identifier, like `pr-251` or `pr-251.0abc123` with a commit hash, and the UTC time, so that every snapshot is unique and they sort by time. Snapshots aren't numbered from tags and can't be combined with `--prerelease`. Like a pre-release, a snapshot writes every version target except the readme `Stable tag`. Build and publish the versioned files without committing them, for example under an npm dist-tag like `pr-251`, since a snapshot consumes the changesets:"},"72":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","type":"page","content":"CLI","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli"},"73":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"CLI"},"74":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"GitHub Actions annotations and job summaries"},"75":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                                    | Description                                   |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release build [<source-dir>] --output <dir> [options]` | Copy selected release files into a directory. |","table":"table-0"},"76":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                   | Description                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release zip [<source-dir>] [options]` | Create a ZIP archive with a `{slug}/` root folder, respecting `.distignore`. |","table":"table-0"},"77":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                         | Description                                                                         |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release checksums [<source-dir>] [options]` | Write the MD5 and SHA-256 hashes of every distributed file to a checksums manifest. |","table":"table-0"},"78":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                               | Description                                                                                                            |\n| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release sign <file>... [options]` | Sign files with Ed25519, writing a detached `<file>.sig` next to each, or minisign and SSH signatures with `--format`. |","table":"table-0"},"79":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command             | Description                                                                                      |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp-release keygen` | Generate a base64 Ed25519 private seed and its public key, also as minisign and SSH public keys. |","table":"table-0"},"80":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                                         | Description                                                                |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release verify [<source-dir>] --checksums <file> [options]` | Check a plugin or theme directory, or a ZIP, against a checksums manifest. |","table":"table-0"},"81":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                             | Description                                                                            |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release version-check [<source-dir>] [options]` | Check that the `Version` header, readme `Stable tag`, PHP constants and git tag agree. |","table":"table-0"},"82":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                          | Description                                                 |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-svn [<source-dir>] [options]` | Deploy a plugin to the WordPress.org plugin SVN repository. |","table":"table-0"},"83":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                                          | Description                                                                             |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-git [<source-dir>] --git-url <url> [options]` | Deploy the files of a plugin, theme or package to a Git repository as a release commit. |","table":"table-0"},"84":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                                                 | Description                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-git --release-ref <ref> [--package <name>] [--list]` | Deploy the configured packages of a release tag or branch to their Git targets. |","table":"table-0"},"85":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                                                 | Description                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-svn --release-ref <ref> [--package <name>] [--list]` | Deploy the configured plugin of a release tag to WordPress.org. |","table":"table-0"},"86":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                        | Description                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-pot [<source-dir>] [options]` | Extract translatable strings into a reproducible POT file. |","table":"table-0"},"87":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                          | Description                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-audit [<source-dir>] [options]` | Report translation string problems without writing files. |","table":"table-0"},"88":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                  | Description                           |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-mo <file.po> [options]` | Compile a translated PO file into MO. |","table":"table-0"},"89":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                                      | Description                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-json <file.po> --domain <domain> [options]` | Compile the JavaScript strings of a translated PO file into WordPress script JSON files. |","table":"table-0"},"90":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                   | Description                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-php <file.po> [options]` | Compile a translated PO file into a WordPress `.l10n.php` file. |","table":"table-0"},"91":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                              | Description                                  |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release changeset-add [options]` | Record the release impact of a pull request. |","table":"table-0"},"92":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                 | Description                                                      |\n| --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release changeset-status [options]` | List pending releases and check changed packages for changesets. |","table":"table-0"},"93":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                                  | Description                                |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp-release changeset-version [options]` | Apply version bumps and changelog entries. |","table":"table-0"},"94":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"| Command                              | Description                          |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |\n| `wp-release changeset-tag [options]` | Create missing package release tags. |","table":"table-0"},"95":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-22","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"Run `npx wp-release <command> --help` for the options of each command. Directory-based commands accept a first `<source-dir>` argument or `--source-dir`, defaulting to the current directory. `sign` and the `i18n-mo`, `i18n-json` and `i18n-php` compilers take file arguments, `keygen` generates keys, and changeset commands operate on the configured repository. Every command prints its result as JSON with `--json`. Commands that take no positional arguments, like the changeset commands, reject bare arguments with the usage instead of ignoring them."},"96":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-23","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"In GitHub Actions, `--github-output` writes the complete result as JSON to `steps.<id>.outputs.result` and exposes each top-level string, number or boolean as a plain output. Use `steps.zip.outputs.zipPath` for a ZIP path, for example. Arrays and objects remain in `result`: tag metadata is read with `fromJSON( steps.tag.outputs.result ).tags[0].tag`, even when only one package is selected. There is no plain `steps.tag.outputs.tag` output."},"97":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-24","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#cli","content":"Prefer plain outputs in steps that run conditionally, since a skipped step has empty outputs. When reading JSON from a step that may be skipped, use a fallback such as `fromJSON( steps.tag.outputs.result || '{\"tags\":[]}' ).tags[0].tag`. For several tags or deploys, read the arrays in `result` to build a job matrix or process each item."},"98":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-25","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"With the `github` log format, the CLI uses current GitHub Actions features to report problems where you look for them, instead of only in the log. `--log-format <name>` picks how the CLI logs, separately from the result that `--json` prints:"},"99":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-26","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Log format | Output                                          |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `text`     | Plain text. The default outside GitHub Actions. |","table":"table-1"},"100":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-27","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Log format | Output                                                                                                              |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `github`   | GitHub Actions annotations and job summaries. The default when `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true`. `--ci` is a shorthand for it. |","table":"table-1"},"101":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-28","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"Pass `--log-format text` for plain text in GitHub Actions. The GitHub Actions always use the `github` log format. Only the CLI checks `GITHUB_ACTIONS`: the library classes don't read the environment, and choose the format by the writer they get."},"102":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-29","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"Errors, warnings and notices are printed as workflow commands, so they show up as annotations on the run and on the files of a pull request:"},"103":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-30","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"`version-check` and `deploy-svn` annotate each version mismatch on the file and line of the version: the `Version` header, the readme `Stable tag` or the PHP constant."},"104":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-31","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"`verify` annotates each changed file and each file that isn't in the checksums."},"105":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-32","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"Each command also adds a section to the job summary of the run, when `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` is set:"},"106":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-33","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command      | Summary                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deploy-svn` | Plugin, linked to its WordPress.org page, version, written SVN paths, the commit linked to its WordPress.org changeset (or the dry run or no-op result), and the SVN changes in a collapsed table. |","table":"table-2"},"107":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-34","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command      | Summary                                                   |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deploy-git` | Repository, release commit and the pushed branch and tag. |","table":"table-2"},"108":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-35","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command | Summary                                                                                               |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `build` | Output directory, number of copied files and optional manifest; requested signatures and public keys. |","table":"table-2"},"109":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-36","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command | Summary                                                                                                                |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `zip`   | ZIP, number of files and checksums manifest; with `--sign`, signature files and the public key of each written format. |","table":"table-2"},"110":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-37","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command     | Summary                       |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `checksums` | Manifest and number of files. |","table":"table-2"},"111":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-38","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command | Summary                                                    |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sign`  | Signature files and the public key of each written format. |","table":"table-2"},"112":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-39","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command  | Summary                                                                                  |\n| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `verify` | That all files match, or the counts and a table of the changed, missing and extra files. |","table":"table-2"},"113":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-40","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command         | Summary                                                                        |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `version-check` | The matching version, or a table of the mismatches with their files and lines. |","table":"table-2"},"114":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-41","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command            | Summary                                               |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-status` | Each changed package with whether it has a changeset. |","table":"table-2"},"115":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-42","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command             | Summary                                                               |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-version` | A table of the package releases with their version changes and bumps. |","table":"table-2"},"116":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-43","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"| Command         | Summary                                                                                  |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-tag` | Each tag with its previous tag and status, and its release notes in a collapsed section. |","table":"table-2"},"117":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-44","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"A failed command adds its error to the summary, or a table of its problems with their files and lines, listing up to 100 and counting the rest. The Markdown of the summaries isn't part of the library API. File paths are relative to `GITHUB_WORKSPACE`, or the current directory without it. Files outside it, like those of an extracted ZIP, get an annotation without a file. Multi-line messages stay one annotation."},"118":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli-45","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/cli#github-actions-annotations-and-job-summaries","content":"Use global `--config <path>` to select a config; relative paths resolve from the current directory. Otherwise, commands using the config discover `wp-release.config.js`, `.mjs` or `.cjs` at the repository root. Global `--tag-format <format>` replaces the `tagFormat` of the config for one command, see Packages and tags."},"119":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","type":"page","content":"Comparison with other tools","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison"},"120":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"Comparison with existing tools"},"121":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool                                                             | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 10up WordPress.org plugin deploy and readme/asset update actions | Two separate GitHub Actions configured through env vars. A deploy always syncs the build to `trunk/` and copies it to the tag; readme and asset updates need the second action. Assets come from `.wordpress-org` by default, and files are selected with `.distignore` or `.gitattributes`. Files are synced with `rsync`. | One `deploy-svn` command with independent write flags, so a release, a readme-only update and an assets-only update are the same command with different flags. Runs locally and in any CI, not only GitHub Actions, and syncs in Node.js without `rsync`. Checks that the `Version` header, readme `Stable tag` and SVN tag agree before writing, which the 10up actions don't. Assets are deployed only when `--assets-dir` is passed, instead of always syncing `.wordpress-org` when it exists, so a plugin's own `assets/` folder is never uploaded by accident. Adds `--exclude` for patterns without an ignore file, opt-in Git `export-ignore` support and `--from-zip` to deploy the exact files of a ZIP. |","table":"table-0"},"122":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool              | What it does                                                                                                     | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grunt-wp-deploy` | Grunt task with `deploy_trunk` and `deploy_tag` booleans, interactive password prompts and a `Stable tag` check. | No Grunt dependency and never interactive. `--readme-only` is a separate flag, where `grunt-wp-deploy` can only update trunk with the full build. Assets are independent of trunk writes. |","table":"table-0"},"123":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool                  | What it does                                                                     | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| codeinwp theme deploy | Fork of the 10up plugin action that commits themes to the themes SVN repository. | Doesn't deploy themes. Theme releases are ZIP uploads on WordPress.org; `zip` builds the upload with the theme folder as the root. |","table":"table-0"},"124":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool              | What it does                                                                                             | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp dist-archive` | WP-CLI command that zips a project using `.distignore`, with `--plugin-dirname` and `--filename-format`. | Same `.distignore` file and `{name}.{version}` style filename format, but needs only Node.js instead of PHP and WP-CLI. `.distignore` is matched with full gitignore syntax, including `!` negation, and `--distignore` / `--exclude` let you point at another ignore file or add patterns on the command line. Builds are reproducible, and `--checksums` writes a manifest next to the ZIP. |","table":"table-0"},"125":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool               | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                        | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp i18n make-pot` | WP-CLI command that extracts PHP, JavaScript, `block.json` and `theme.json` strings into a POT, with `--domain`, `--include`/`--exclude`, `--headers`, `--file-comment`, `--merge`/`--subtract` and audit warnings. | `i18n-pot` and `i18n-audit` need only Node.js, write byte-for-byte reproducible POTs without dates, and `--check` fails CI on a stale POT. Headers are set one at a time with a repeatable `--header NAME=VALUE` instead of a JSON object, and `--include`/`--exclude` take one glob each, so patterns never need escaping or comma splitting. `--no-references` and `--all-domains` say what they do. Monorepos keep these settings once in `wp-release.config.js` and select packages with `--package` or `--all` instead of running the command for every directory. The audit is a separate read-only command with stable finding codes for `--suppress`, instead of warnings mixed into generation. There's no `--merge`, `--subtract` or `--skip-*`; `--include` and `--exclude` cover the same needs. |","table":"table-0"},"126":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool                                          | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                          | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp i18n make-mo`, `make-json` and `make-php` | WP-CLI commands that compile PO files into MO, script JSON and `.l10n.php` files. `make-json` maps sources with `--use-map` and removes JavaScript strings from the PO unless `--no-purge` is passed. | `i18n-mo`, `i18n-json` and `i18n-php` compile one translated PO at a time, never change it, and leave out fuzzy, obsolete and untranslated entries. `i18n-json` takes a many-to-many `--map` from source files to built scripts, and JSX and TypeScript sources need one, so catalogs are never written for files WordPress doesn't load. `--check` compares the outputs without writing. There's no `update-po`, so update PO files from the POT with gettext's `msgmerge` or a translation platform. |","table":"table-0"},"127":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool                         | What it does                                                                          | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp plugin verify-checksums` | WP-CLI command that checks installed plugins against the WordPress.org checksums API. | Reads the same manifest format, but from a file, so it also works for plugins and themes that aren't hosted on WordPress.org and for build directories in CI. `checksums` writes the manifest that WordPress.org generates for its own plugins. |","table":"table-0"},"128":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/comparison#comparison-with-existing-tools","content":"| Tool                    | What it does                                                                                                                         | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-scripts plugin-zip` | Zips files from `package.json` `files` or a fixed allowlist, named after the npm package, with `--root-folder` / `--no-root-folder`. | Reuses the root folder flags, selects files with `.distignore` by default and can optionally honor Git `export-ignore` attributes, puts the version from the plugin headers in the filename and streams the archive instead of building it in memory. |","table":"table-0"},"129":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","type":"page","content":"Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration"},"130":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"Naming convention"},"131":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"File selection"},"132":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#plugin-and-theme-headers","content":"Plugin and theme headers"},"133":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"`@wpelevator/wp-release` is the suite of release tools and `wp-release` is its CLI. \"Release\" is the whole workflow; each command is one operation in it. Commands are flat: a single word or dashed words like `version-check` and `deploy-svn`, without command groups or aliases. Each GitHub Action is named `wp-release-` followed by the command name, so the CLI command and the `uses:` name always match:"},"134":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability        | CLI command        | GitHub Action |\n| ----------------- | ------------------ | ------------- |\n| Build a directory | `wp-release build` |               |","table":"table-0"},"135":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability  | CLI command      | GitHub Action               |\n| ----------- | ---------------- | --------------------------- |\n| Build a ZIP | `wp-release zip` | `wpelevator/wp-release-zip` |","table":"table-0"},"136":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability                  | CLI command            | GitHub Action                     |\n| --------------------------- | ---------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Create a checksums manifest | `wp-release checksums` | `wpelevator/wp-release-checksums` |","table":"table-0"},"137":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability | CLI command       | GitHub Action                |\n| ---------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Sign files | `wp-release sign` | `wpelevator/wp-release-sign` |","table":"table-0"},"138":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability                      | CLI command         | GitHub Action |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------- | ------------- |\n| Verify files against a manifest | `wp-release verify` |               |","table":"table-0"},"139":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability                    | CLI command                | GitHub Action |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------- |\n| Check that the versions agree | `wp-release version-check` |               |","table":"table-0"},"140":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability                  | CLI command             | GitHub Action                      |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| Deploy to WordPress.org SVN | `wp-release deploy-svn` | `wpelevator/wp-release-deploy-svn` |","table":"table-0"},"141":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Capability                 | CLI command             | GitHub Action |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------- |\n| Deploy to a Git repository | `wp-release deploy-git` |               |","table":"table-0"},"142":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"The names below are scoped to their commands. `--output` selects the primary artifact of each command; ZIP packaging uses `--checksums-file` for its accompanying manifest:"},"143":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept                   | CLI                                       | GitHub Action | Library     | Meaning                                                                           |\n| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Plugin or theme directory | `<source-dir>` argument or `--source-dir` | `source-dir`  | `sourceDir` | Directory that a command reads, whether it's the repository or a build directory. |","table":"table-1"},"144":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept         | CLI                    | GitHub Action | Library     | Meaning                                                    |\n| --------------- | ---------------------- | ------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Build directory | `build --output <dir>` | —             | `outputDir` | Directory written by `build`; files go directly inside it. |","table":"table-1"},"145":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept  | CLI                   | GitHub Action          | Library   | Meaning                                                           |\n| -------- | --------------------- | ---------------------- | --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| ZIP file | `zip --output <file>` | `output` in ZIP action | `zipPath` | Destination passed to `zipPackage()`; also returned as `zipPath`. |","table":"table-1"},"146":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept                   | CLI                         | GitHub Action                | Library                              | Meaning                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------- | --------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Standalone checksums file | `checksums --output <file>` | `output` in checksums action | first argument of `writeChecksums()` | Manifest destination relative to the working directory; the CLI returns its absolute path as `checksumsPath`. |","table":"table-1"},"147":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept                         | CLI                           | GitHub Action                  | Library         | Meaning                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------ | --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Checksums filename when zipping | `zip --checksums-file <file>` | `checksums-file` in ZIP action | `checksumsFile` | Relative manifest filename beside the ZIP or inside its package root when embedded. |","table":"table-1"},"148":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept       | CLI                 | GitHub Action | Library   | Meaning                                 |\n| ------------- | ------------------- | ------------- | --------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| ZIP to deploy | `--from-zip <file>` | `from-zip`    | `fromZip` | Existing ZIP read as the deploy source. |","table":"table-1"},"149":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept            | CLI               | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning                             |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| Git tag to compare | `--git-tag <tag>` |               | `gitTag` | Git tag checked by `version-check`. |","table":"table-1"},"150":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept           | CLI                   | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning                                               |\n| ----------------- | --------------------- | ------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| SVN tag to create | `--svn-tag <version>` | `svn-tag`     | `svnTag` | Tag written by `deploy-svn`, distinct from a git tag. |","table":"table-1"},"151":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept        | CLI               | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning             |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------- | -------- | ------------------- |\n| SVN repository | `--svn-url <url>` | `svn-url`     | `svnUrl` | Deploy destination. |","table":"table-1"},"152":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-22","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept        | CLI               | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning                                                                                                               |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Git repository | `--git-url <url>` |               | `gitUrl` | Destination of `deploy-git`. Credentials come from git, like a token in the URL or an SSH key, and are never printed. |","table":"table-1"},"153":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-23","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept                      | CLI                               | GitHub Action | Library         | Meaning                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Branch and tag to release to | `--branch <name>`, `--tag <name>` |               | `branch`, `tag` | Refs written by `deploy-git` in the Git repository, distinct from the git tag that `version-check` compares. |","table":"table-1"},"154":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-24","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept        | CLI                    | GitHub Action | Library       | Meaning                                                                                                                       |\n| -------------- | ---------------------- | ------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Source history | `--split-prefix <dir>` |               | `splitPrefix` | Directory of a monorepo package whose history, from `git subtree split`, becomes the source history of `deploy-git` releases. |","table":"table-1"},"155":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-25","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept         | CLI                                                                 | GitHub Action                  | Library                      | Meaning                                                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| SVN credentials | `--svn-username <name>`, `SVN_USERNAME` and `SVN_PASSWORD` env vars | `svn-username`, `svn-password` | `svnUsername`, `svnPassword` | Needed only to commit. There's no password flag, since it would show in the process list; the action inputs and library options fall back to the env vars. |","table":"table-1"},"156":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-26","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept    | CLI                                                | GitHub Action   | Library                                                   | Meaning                                                                                                                               |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Log format | `--log-format <text\\|github>`, `--ci` for `github` | always `github` | `writer: new TextWriter()` or `new GitHubActionsWriter()` | How the log is printed: plain text, or GitHub Actions annotations and job summaries. Each name matches the start of its writer class. |","table":"table-1"},"157":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-27","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept        | CLI               | GitHub Action                              | Library      | Meaning                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Release config | `--config <path>` | `config` in ZIP, checksums and SVN actions | `configFile` | Explicit JavaScript config path for package selection, integrity settings or deployment. |","table":"table-1"},"158":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-28","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept            | CLI                     | GitHub Action              | Library     | Meaning                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------- | -------------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Release tag format | `--tag-format <format>` | `tag-format` in SVN action | `tagFormat` | How `release-ref` identifies a package and its version. |","table":"table-1"},"159":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-29","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept               | CLI                   | GitHub Action               | Library | Meaning                                                             |\n| --------------------- | --------------------- | --------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Release ref to deploy | `--release-ref <ref>` | `release-ref` in SVN action | `ref`   | Read configured packages and deployment targets from a release ref. |","table":"table-1"},"160":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-30","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"| Concept     | CLI                   | GitHub Action           | Library  | Meaning                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------- | --------------------- | ----------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Assets sync | `--no-assets` to skip | `assets: false` to skip | `assets` | Booleans are positive in the library and the action; `--no-assets` is only the CLI negation, never `noAssets` or a `no-assets` input. |","table":"table-1"},"161":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-31","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"CLI JSON results use camelCase keys like `zipPath`, and GitHub Action outputs use kebab-case like `zip-path`. The ZIP action supports `package` and `config`, the checksums action supports `config` for codec selection, and the SVN action supports `release-ref`, repeated `package` values as newline-separated input, `list`, `config` and `tag-format`."},"162":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-32","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"The ZIP CLI result uses `checksumsEmbedded` for the package-relative embedded manifest path. Its `signatures` array includes external absolute paths and embedded package-relative paths, while `checksumsSignatures` contains only manifest signatures. The ZIP action exposes those locations as `checksums-embedded`, `signatures` for external signatures, and `signatures-embedded` for embedded signatures, so asset uploads receive local files. With CLI `--github-output`, signature arrays remain in the JSON `result` output."},"163":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-33","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#naming-convention","content":"Library classes are named after the capability (`SvnDeploy`, `Svn`, `GitDeploy`) without repeating \"release\"."},"164":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-34","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"`build`, `zip`, `checksums`, `deploy-svn` and `deploy-git` select files the same way:"},"165":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-35","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"| Option                | Description                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `--distignore <file>` | Path to the ignore file. Defaults to `.distignore` in the source directory when present; an explicitly passed file must exist. |","table":"table-2"},"166":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-36","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"| Option            | Description                 |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `--no-distignore` | Don't read any ignore file. |","table":"table-2"},"167":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-37","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"| Option                 | Description                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--exclude <patterns>` | Extra gitignore-style patterns, comma-separated or repeated, like `--exclude build/,assets/*,images/**/*`. They're applied after the ignore file, so a `!` pattern can also re-include a file. |","table":"table-2"},"168":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-38","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"| Option                    | Description                                                                                                                                  |\n| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--exclude-gitattributes` | Also exclude paths marked `export-ignore` by Git attributes. This is opt-in and requires the source directory to be inside a Git repository. |","table":"table-2"},"169":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-39","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#file-selection","content":"`.distignore` and `--exclude` use full gitignore syntax, including `!` negation. `--exclude-gitattributes` asks Git to evaluate `export-ignore` attributes, including nested `.gitattributes` files, then adds those paths to the exclusions. It is off by default, so releases don't unexpectedly depend on Git or change selection when a repository adds attributes. Git must be installed and the source directory must be inside a working tree; the flag is ignored by `deploy-svn --from-zip`, since the ZIP is already filtered. Version control metadata (`.git`, `.svn` and `.hg`, at any depth, including the `.git` files of worktrees and submodules) is always left out, also without an ignore file and when deploying from a ZIP, and no `!` pattern can re-include it. This keeps a repository's history out of ZIPs and deploys when the source directory is the repository root. The comma-separated `--exclude` list works like `--exclude` in `wp i18n make-pot`. Selected symbolic links are an error, since ZIP archives and WordPress.org SVN don't support them."},"170":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-40","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#plugin-and-theme-headers","content":"The commands read the same headers as WordPress. The main plugin file is the top-level `.php` file with a `Plugin Name:` header in its first 8 KB, preferring `{slug}.php` when several files have one. A directory without a plugin file and with `Theme Name:` in `style.css` is a theme. The readme headers (`Stable tag`, `Tested up to`, …) come from `readme.txt`, or `readme.md` when there's no `readme.txt`."},"171":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration-41","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/configuration#plugin-and-theme-headers","content":"`--version` defaults to the `Version` header in `zip`, `checksums` and `version-check`, and `checksums --type` defaults to the detected package type."},"172":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","type":"page","content":"Deployment","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment"},"173":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"Deploy to SVN"},"174":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"Deploy to Git"},"175":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"Deploy configured packages"},"176":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"The `wp-release-deploy-svn` GitHub Action runs this command."},"177":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"`deploy-svn` syncs a plugin to the WordPress.org plugin SVN repository. Each SVN write is a separate flag and nothing is written unless requested. All writes go into one commit."},"178":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"| Flag      | Default | Writes                                     |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `--trunk` | off     | Sync the full source directory to `trunk/` |","table":"table-0"},"179":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"| Flag            | Default | Writes                                                                                                                             |\n| --------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--readme-only` | off     | Sync only the readme to `trunk/`, for updates between releases such as bumping `Tested up to`. Can't be combined with `--svn-tag`. |","table":"table-0"},"180":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"| Flag                  | Default | Writes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| --------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--svn-tag <version>` | off     | Create `tags/<version>`. With `--trunk`, the tag is a copy of the synced `trunk/`. Without it, the tag is built directly from the source files and `trunk/` is left alone, for pre-release tags. A leading `v` is stripped, so `v1.2.0` creates `tags/1.2.0`. |","table":"table-0"},"181":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"| Flag                 | Default | Writes                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| -------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--assets-dir <dir>` | not set | Sync this directory with banners, icons and screenshots to the SVN `assets/` directory. There's no default directory, so assets are only deployed when this is set, and it must exist. |","table":"table-0"},"182":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"| Flag          | Default | Writes                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--no-assets` |         | Skip the `--assets-dir` sync for one run, for example when a config always passes `--assets-dir`. |","table":"table-0"},"183":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"Typical runs:"},"184":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"How a deploy works:"},"185":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"The options are validated, including the slug, version and readme name, before they're used in URLs or paths."},"186":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"With `--trunk` or `--svn-tag`, the files must be a plugin whose `Version` header matches the version. When `--trunk` and `--svn-tag` are combined, the readme `Stable tag` must match too; a tag-only run skips that, since a pre-release tag doesn't change the stable tag. `--readme-only` needs the readme. Nothing is checked out when a check fails."},"187":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"The repository is checked out sparsely into a new temporary directory, with only the directories that the flags write to. A missing `assets/` (or `trunk/`, `tags/`) directory is created. The deploy stops when `tags/<version>` exists, unless `--force` replaces it."},"188":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"The files are synced in Node.js, without `rsync`: new and changed files are copied, files that are no longer selected are deleted, and `svn add` and `svn delete` stage them, including files that svn ignores by default. With `--trunk`, `trunk/` is then copied to the tag with `svn copy`. Without it, the same selected files are synced into `tags/<version>` instead, and with `--force` an existing tag is updated to match, deleting files that are no longer in the build."},"189":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"Asset images get `svn:mime-type` set for PNG, JPEG, GIF and SVG, the formats that WordPress.org documents for plugin assets. Other formats keep the type that svn assigns."},"190":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"When `svn status` is empty, the deploy ends without a commit. A dry run logs the status and stops before the commit. Otherwise the changes are committed, and the temporary directory is always removed."},"191":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"`--from-zip` reads the files from a ZIP instead of `<source-dir>`. The ZIP is checked before it's extracted: entries with absolute paths, `..` segments or symbolic links, and entries outside a single `{slug}/` root folder, are rejected."},"192":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"Credentials are only needed for the commit, not for dry runs, no-op runs or `file://` repositories. Pass the username with `--svn-username` or `SVN_USERNAME` and the password in `SVN_PASSWORD`. svn always runs non-interactively without caching credentials, and the password is passed on the standard input with `--password-from-stdin`, never on the command line or in the logs."},"193":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"Validation:"},"194":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"`--readme-only` can't be combined with `--svn-tag`, since a tag is a release snapshot that a readme update never changes."},"195":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-22","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"`--version` defaults to `--svn-tag`, then the `Version` header of the deployed files; when `--version` and `--svn-tag` are both set they must match."},"196":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-23","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"Pre-release versions like `1.0.0-rc.1` can be tagged, but syncing one to `trunk/` warns, since trunk is for the development version."},"197":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-24","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"`--trunk` without `--svn-tag` works but warns, to remind you that updating trunk alone doesn't create a version tag for stable downloads."},"198":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-25","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-svn","content":"A version mismatch throws a `VersionMismatchError`, a `ValidationError` with the `mismatches` and a problem for each, with its file and line. See Logging."},"199":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-26","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"`deploy-git` deploys the selected files of a plugin, theme or package to a Git repository, like a distribution repository that ships the built files and `vendor/` for Composer installs or GitHub-hosted updates, or the release repository of a monorepo package. It selects files like `zip`, with `.distignore` and `--exclude`, so built files that git ignores in the source repository, like `build/` or `vendor/`, are released too. The source directory must be inside a Git repository, which provides the source history and stores the release objects before they're pushed."},"200":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-27","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"| Flag              | Default | Writes                          |\n| ----------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `--branch <name>` | off     | Push the release to this branch |","table":"table-1"},"201":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-28","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"| Flag           | Default | Writes                          |\n| -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `--tag <name>` | off     | Create this tag for the release |","table":"table-1"},"202":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-29","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"How a deploy works:"},"203":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-30","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"The selected files are written to the object database of the source repository and become a tree, with a temporary index, so the working tree and the index of the repository are left alone. Executable files stay executable."},"204":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-31","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"The previous release is fetched: the tip of `--branch`, or for a tag-only deploy the tip of the repository's default branch (its `HEAD`), whatever that branch holds. Release a tag together with `--branch` to choose the branch it builds on."},"205":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-32","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"When the files are the same as in the previous release, that release is reused. Otherwise a release commit is created with the files, whose parents are the previous release and the source history: the source commit (`--source-ref`, default `HEAD`), or with `--split-prefix` the history of that directory from `git subtree split`. The repository keeps the source history, and `git log --first-parent` lists the releases."},"206":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-33","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"When the previous release on `--branch` already has newer source history, the branch isn't moved, so running an older build again, like a re-run of an earlier CI job, can't release older files. The source history is outdated when it's an older commit of the source history that the previous release was made from, its last parent. `git subtree split` makes this reliable for `--split-prefix` too, since it creates the same commits for the same history. A revert is a new commit, so it's released, and with `--tag` the tag of an older build is still released, without moving the branch."},"207":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-34","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"The tag is checked in the repository first. A tag that already has the same files is the release: it's reused and nothing is pushed, so running a tag release again is a no-op. A tag with other files fails with exit code `2` before anything is pushed."},"208":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-35","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"The release is pushed to the branch, unless it didn't change or is outdated, and to the tag, in one atomic push. Pushes are never forced, so the branch only moves forward and a tag is never replaced, also when it was created in the meantime."},"209":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-36","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"To release a tag again, for example after a release with the wrong files, delete the tag in the repository (`git push <url> :refs/tags/<tag>`) and run the deploy again. There's no flag for replacing a tag, since installs, Composer and GitHub Actions pins trust that a published tag doesn't change."},"210":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-37","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-to-git","content":"`--dry-run` runs everything except the push. `--message` sets the commit message, which defaults to `Release <tag or branch> from <source commit>`. Credentials come from git, like a credential helper or an SSH key, and credentials in URLs are never printed. For an HTTPS repository on `github.com`, or on the host of `GITHUB_SERVER_URL` like GitHub Enterprise Server, the `GITHUB_TOKEN` environment variable is sent as an authorization header, like `actions/checkout` does, so the token stays out of the URL, the git arguments and the repository config, and it replaces the header that `actions/checkout` persists. Pass `token` to `resolveGitDeployOptions()` for another HTTPS host. The commit needs a git identity (`user.name` and `user.email`)."},"211":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-38","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"`deploy-git --release-ref <ref>` and `deploy-svn --release-ref <ref>` deploy the packages of a monorepo, or of a single-package repository, to the targets in the `deploy` config of their package groups, like the release repository of each package or its WordPress.org plugin. The ref decides what's deployed: a release tag of a package, like `refs/tags/plugins/alpha/1.2.0` read with the `tagFormat`, deploys that package and version, and a branch, like `refs/heads/main`, deploys every package with a target for that branch. A name without `refs/` is a release tag when it matches the tag format of a package, otherwise a branch, and in GitHub Actions `$GITHUB_REF` is the pushed ref."},"212":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-39","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"| Mode | Command                          | Use                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ---- | -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| All  | `deploy-git --release-ref <ref>` | Deploy every package of the ref to each of its targets, one at a time, in one log group for each package. A failed deploy doesn't stop the others, and the command exits with `1` at the end. |","table":"table-2"},"213":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-40","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"| Mode        | Command                                           | Use                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| One package | `deploy-git --release-ref <ref> --package <name>` | Deploy only that package (repeatable), by identifier or path, for example to retry a failed one or in a CI job for each package with its own credentials. |","table":"table-2"},"214":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-41","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"| Mode | Command                                 | Use                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ---- | --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Plan | `deploy-git --release-ref <ref> --list` | List the deploys without running them, with each package, its version or branch and the targets with the tag or branch they get. With `--json` or `--github-output` the result is `{ packages: [ … ] }`, for a CI matrix with `include: ${{ fromJSON( steps.plan.outputs.result ).packages }}`. |","table":"table-2"},"215":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-42","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"| Mode             | Command                                      | Use                                                        |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Single directory | `deploy-git <dir> --git-url <url> [options]` | Deploy one directory without a config, as described above. |","table":"table-2"},"216":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-43","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"`deploy-svn` has the same modes. `--release-ref` can't be combined with the options of a single target, like `--git-url`, `--tag` or `--svn-tag`, which come from the config."},"217":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-44","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Git targets** take a `repository` and release both branches and tags by default. `branches` limits the branches, like `[ 'main' ]`, or turns them off with `false`, and `tag` is the tag template in the repository, `{version}` by default, or `false` to not release tags. A release tag is created on top of the default branch of the repository, and a branch is released to the same branch. `history: false` uses the source commit instead of the package history from `git subtree split`, and `message` sets the release commit message, `Release {ref} from {revision}` by default."},"218":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-45","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**SVN targets** only deploy release tags: a stable release syncs `trunk/`, creates its tag and syncs the `assetsDir`, and a pre-release only creates its tag. `slug`, the WordPress.org plugin slug, defaults to the name of the package directory, `url` to the WordPress.org plugin repository, and `message` sets the commit message. Credentials come from `SVN_USERNAME` and `SVN_PASSWORD`, or `--svn-username`."},"219":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-46","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Several targets**: `git` and `svn` each take one target or a list, like a release repository and a mirror on another host. Each target is a separate deploy."},"220":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-47","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Credentials**: a GitHub repository URL needs no token in it, since `GITHUB_TOKEN` is sent as an authorization header (see above). A callback can still add credentials for other hosts."},"221":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-48","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Templates and callbacks**: `repository`, `slug` and `message` are templates with `{name}` and `{path}`, like the `tagFormat`, and `{version}`, `{branch}`, `{ref}` (the tag or branch that the deploy writes in the target) and `{revision}` (the source commit), or callbacks that receive `{ package, version, branch, ref, revision }`, where `package` has the `name`, `directory`, `path` and `type`. A callback can name a target after the package directory, choose another host for some packages, read the environment, or return nothing to skip the target for that package or ref; an empty `message` uses the default one. Credentials in URLs are never printed or listed."},"222":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-49","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Results**: with `--json` or `--github-output`, the result lists each deploy in `deploys` with its `package`, `label`, `target` (the repository or WordPress.org plugin, with credentials redacted), `targetRef` (the tag or branch written there), `version` or `branch`, and its `result` or `error`. A remote target repository also has its `targetHost`, like `github.com`, and `targetPath`, like `me/my-plugin`, without credentials, for a workflow step that calls the API of the forge that hosts it, such as to create the release of the deployed tag. The targets of `--list` have the same `host` and `path`, and `parseRepositoryUrl()` reads them from an HTTP(S), SSH or scp-like URL. `wp-release` doesn't call forge APIs itself."},"223":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-50","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Packages without a version**: a package entry without `version`, like a single directory with a `glob` of its path, isn't released with changesets, so changes to it don't need one, and it only deploys branches, since it has no release tags."},"224":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-51","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"**Files**: `files.exclude` adds gitignore-style patterns relative to the package directory, and `files.distignore` sets the ignore file relative to it or turns it off with `false`. A `files` at the root of the config applies to every package group, for the rules that all packages share, and the `files` of a group add their patterns after it and can replace its ignore file. Without `files`, each package's `.distignore` applies, like for `zip`; there's no repository-wide `.distignore`, since each one is relative to its package. `zip --package <name>` zips a package with the same files, `getPackageFiles()` returns the directory and the file selection of any configured package, and `getDeployPackages()` lists the packages with deploy targets and their file selection."},"225":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-52","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"A deploy releases the files of the working tree, so build the packages first. One job that builds once and runs `deploy-git --release-ref \"$GITHUB_REF\"` is the simplest setup, also for a branch that releases every package. A matrix with a job for each package from `--list` would build every package in every job, so build once and pass the built tree to the jobs as an artifact if you need one, for example for separate credentials."},"226":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment-53","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/deployment#deploy-configured-packages","content":"Other monorepo tools split the same way with less of the release in the config: splitsh-lite, Symplify MonorepoBuilder and `danharrin/monorepo-split-github-action` push the source history of each package directory, with the packages and repositories listed in the workflow or a matrix, while `deploy-git` releases the built files on top of that history, so `vendor/` and `build/` reach Composer and WordPress installs. Like the publishers of GoReleaser, targets are lists with templates, and pre-releases skip the targets where they would replace the stable version."},"227":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","type":"page","content":"Development","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development"},"228":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"Development"},"229":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"Architecture"},"230":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"The source is TypeScript in `src/`, with tests next to the files they test (`*.test.ts`). The published package contains only the compiled `dist/` with JavaScript and `.d.ts` files; tests and translation fixtures stay in the repository."},"231":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"`build` compiles `src/` to `dist/` with `tsc`, leaving out the tests."},"232":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"`test` runs the `.ts` tests directly with `node --test`, using Node.js type stripping, so it needs Node.js 22.18 or later but no build. The SVN integration tests in `svn-deploy.integration.test.ts` create repositories with `svnadmin` and deploy to their `file://` URLs; they're skipped when `svn` or `svnadmin` isn't installed."},"233":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"`lint` runs ESLint and a `tsc` type check."},"234":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"The i18n tests validate gettext round trips, script catalogs and generated PHP data without launching PHP, WP-CLI or WordPress, covering contexts, plural forms, comments, flags, Unicode, escaping and PHP-looking input as data."},"235":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"The `wpelevator-source` export condition points at `src/index.ts`. The wp-release tests and monorepo CLI scripts use it to work with the source instead of a `dist/` build. Action builds use the declared dependency's default export, which resolves to `dist/` for the workspace copy and the published package. Build wp-release before building the actions."},"236":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#development","content":"`execute( 'deploy-svn', args, { cwd, logger, svnRunner } )` runs a single-plugin deployment or a configured plan or deployment. The optional `svnRunner` replaces the external command runner in both modes; without it, the command uses its normal runner."},"237":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"wp-release has two layers, so that it fits any repository layout:"},"238":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"**Primitives** work on the directory, file or values they're given and know nothing about the repository: `build <dir>`, `zip <dir>`, `checksums`, `sign`, `verify`, `version-check`, `deploy-git <dir>`, `deploy-svn <dir>`, `i18n-pot <dir>`, `i18n-audit <dir>` and the `i18n-mo`, `i18n-json` and `i18n-php` compilers."},"239":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"**The config layer** reads `wp-release.config.js` for everything about the repository: where packages are (`root` and the package globs), how their versions and tags are named (`version`, `tagFormat`), which files they ship (`files`), how their translations are extracted (`i18n`), where they deploy (`deploy`) and the git `remote`. The changeset commands (with a single-project fallback when no config exists), `deploy-git` and `deploy-svn --release-ref`, `build --package` and `zip --package`, and `i18n-pot` and `i18n-audit` with `--package` or `--all` belong to it, and the global `--config` and `--tag-format` options select or adjust the config. `zip` and `checksums` also read caller configuration for integrity settings even without `--package`; verification reads it only when explicitly selected. Only `--release-ref` reads which package and version a ref releases. A primitive with a config mode, like `deploy-git --release-ref` beside `deploy-git <dir>` or `zip --package` beside `zip <dir>`, only takes the package directory, its files and its targets from the config there, and otherwise works the same."},"240":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"Configured repositories use the layout and naming conventions their config sets. Config-free changeset releases use the root-only detection and defaults described above."},"241":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"The GitHub Actions are adapters to these commands: they use the same flag names and `execute()` entry point, with secrets supplied through environment variables. The SVN action injects a command runner that checks or installs SVN only when a deployment runs its first SVN command; config planning and deployment behavior remain in `wp-release`."},"242":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"The package uses ES modules as its unit of encapsulation, like most Node.js CLI tools: a module exports a few functions and keeps its helpers private, so there's no class to wrap them in. A class is used only when it holds state across steps or binds dependencies, so that callers and tests can replace them:"},"243":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"**Workflows with steps** are classes, so that their steps share state and dependencies on the instance: `SvnDeploy` shares the temporary working copy across `prepare()`, `checkout()`, `sync()` and `commit()`, `GitDeploy` binds the options and the git runner to its steps, and `ChangesetRelease` binds the repository and the runners to `add()`, `version()` and `tag()`."},"244":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"**External commands** run through injectable runners: `createSvnRunner()` and `createGitRunner()` return functions that spawn the binary without a shell. The classes take a runner as an option and create the default one when it's missing, so tests pass a fake runner, or one that wraps the real runner and records some commands, instead of putting fake binaries on the `PATH`. The `Svn` and `ReleaseRepository` classes wrap a runner with the commands of their domain."},"245":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"**Domain logic** is plain functions that take their dependencies as arguments, like `discoverPackages( repository, config )`, so that each one can be tested on its own."},"246":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"**Commands** in `src/commands/` parse the CLI options and call the library, and `cli.ts` handles the global options, logging and exit codes. Errors carry their exit code, like `ValidationError` with `2`."},"247":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"Constructors only store their options. Work starts in a method call."},"248":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"The changeset release modules, from the entry point down:"},"249":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                 | Responsibility                                                                                                                              |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-release.ts` | `ChangesetRelease`, which plans releases and runs `add()`, `getStatus()`, `version()` and `tag()`, and the function form of each operation. |","table":"table-0"},"250":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-22","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module              | Responsibility                                                                                       |\n| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `single-project.ts` | Resolving the config-free root project, its version source and targets, and shared release settings. |","table":"table-0"},"251":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-23","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                | Responsibility                                                                            |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-config.ts` | Loading and validating `wp-release.config.js`, and the version source and target helpers. |","table":"table-0"},"252":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-24","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                                             |\n| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-packages.ts` | Discovering the packages of the configured globs, with or without a version, and finding the changed ones. |","table":"table-0"},"253":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-25","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module               | Responsibility                                  |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-files.ts` | Reading, writing and consuming changeset files. |","table":"table-0"},"254":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-26","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                    | Responsibility                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-dependents.ts` | The dependents policy: which packages re-release with their sources, and rewriting their dependency specs. |","table":"table-0"},"255":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-27","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module          | Responsibility                                            |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changesets.ts` | Parsing and formatting changesets, and the release types. |","table":"table-0"},"256":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-28","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module             | Responsibility                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `version-files.ts` | Reading and writing versions in manifests, headers, readmes and PHP constants. |","table":"table-0"},"257":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-29","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module         | Responsibility                                                           |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `changelog.ts` | Changelog entries, and the `CHANGELOG.md` and readme changelog sections. |","table":"table-0"},"258":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-30","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module            | Responsibility                                                                      |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `release-tags.ts` | Choosing the version and previous tag of a release, and building its release notes. |","table":"table-0"},"259":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-31","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module          | Responsibility                            |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `tag-format.ts` | Formatting tag names from the tag format. |","table":"table-0"},"260":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-32","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module              | Responsibility                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `package-deploy.ts` | Resolving the configured deploys of a ref: the released packages, their targets and file selections, for `deploy-git` and `deploy-svn`, and the file selection of a package for `zip --package`. |","table":"table-0"},"261":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-33","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module           | Responsibility                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `package-zip.ts` | `zipPackage()`, the ZIP and checksums manifest of the selected files of a package, for `zip` and the snapshot builds of a monorepo. |","table":"table-0"},"262":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-34","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `release-repository.ts` | `ReleaseRepository`, the git commands of a release: tags, commits and changed files. |","table":"table-0"},"263":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-35","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module            | Responsibility                                                                     |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `file-journal.ts` | `FileJournal`, which restores every file a failed `changeset-version` run changed. |","table":"table-0"},"264":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-36","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module        | Responsibility                        |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `versions.ts` | Version checks and SemVer precedence. |","table":"table-0"},"265":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-37","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"The internationalization modules in `src/i18n/`, from the entry points down:"},"266":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-38","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module        | Responsibility                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `packages.ts` | `resolveI18nPackages()`, the config adapter that selects a directory, one package or all packages and resolves their options and POT paths. |","table":"table-1"},"267":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-39","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module       | Responsibility                                                              |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `options.ts` | Validating and merging the `i18n` options of the config layers and the CLI. |","table":"table-1"},"268":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-40","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module        | Responsibility                                                                                                        |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `generate.ts` | `extractCatalog()` and `generatePackagePot()`, which read the selected files of a package and write or check its POT. |","table":"table-1"},"269":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-41","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module       | Responsibility                                                                                                                |\n| ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `compile.ts` | `compileTranslations()`, which compiles a translated PO into MO, script JSON or `.l10n.php` and writes or checks the outputs. |","table":"table-1"},"270":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-42","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module     | Responsibility                                                                                           |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `files.ts` | Selecting source files with the include and exclude globs, independently of the distribution file rules. |","table":"table-1"},"271":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-43","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                                      | Responsibility                                                                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `php.ts`, `javascript.ts` and `metadata.ts` | Parsers for PHP, JavaScript and TypeScript, and `block.json`, `theme.json` and plugin and theme headers. |","table":"table-1"},"272":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-44","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module          | Responsibility                                                        |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `extraction.ts` | The translation functions and their arguments, shared by the parsers. |","table":"table-1"},"273":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-45","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module       | Responsibility                                                   |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `catalog.ts` | Catalog types, merging messages of a domain and the diagnostics. |","table":"table-1"},"274":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-46","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                           | Responsibility                                               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `audit.ts` and `placeholders.ts` | Audit findings and the sprintf placeholders that they check. |","table":"table-1"},"275":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-47","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module   | Responsibility                  |\n| -------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `pot.ts` | Serializing a catalog as a POT. |","table":"table-1"},"276":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-48","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module                                                  | Responsibility                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `po.ts`, `translation-json.ts` and `translation-php.ts` | Parsing translated PO files and compiling MO, script JSON and `.l10n.php`. |","table":"table-1"},"277":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development-49","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/development#architecture","content":"| Module             | Responsibility                                                 |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `atomic-output.ts` | Validating output paths and replacing output files atomically. |","table":"table-1"},"278":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","type":"page","content":"WP Release","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest"},"279":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest","content":"Build and release WordPress plugins, themes, and packages."},"280":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest#install","content":"Install"},"281":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest#start-with-the-cli","content":"Start with the CLI"},"282":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Guides"},"283":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest","content":"`@wpelevator/wp-release` provides a CLI and library for building release directories and ZIPs, creating checksums and signatures, checking versions, extracting and compiling translations, and deploying to WordPress.org SVN or Git repositories."},"284":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest","content":"The `wp-release-zip`, `wp-release-checksums`, `wp-release-sign`, and `wp-release-deploy-svn` GitHub Actions expose the same commands in workflows."},"285":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest","content":"WordPress.org themes are released by uploading a ZIP, so `deploy-svn` is for plugins only. Use `zip` to build a theme upload. Packaging and deployment options follow familiar community tools to ease migration; the comparison explains the differences."},"286":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#install","content":"Node.js 22.12 or later is required. SVN deployments also require the `svn` command line client. Git deployments and changeset commands require Git."},"287":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#install","content":"The package is an ES module with TypeScript declarations. CommonJS projects can load it with `require( '@wpelevator/wp-release' )` on Node.js 22.12 or later."},"288":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"CLI and automation"},"289":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Changeset releases"},"290":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Configuration and file selection"},"291":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Packaging, checksums, signatures, and verification"},"292":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Git and WordPress.org deployment"},"293":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"WordPress internationalization"},"294":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Library API"},"295":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Development and architecture"},"296":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest#guides","content":"Comparison with existing tools"},"297":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","type":"page","content":"Internationalization","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization"},"298":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#wordpress-internationalization","content":"WordPress internationalization"},"299":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#supported-extraction-syntax","content":"Supported extraction syntax"},"300":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#compile-translated-catalogs","content":"Compile translated catalogs"},"301":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#wordpress-internationalization","content":"`i18n-pot` extracts plugin/theme strings and writes a reproducible UTF-8 POT. `i18n-audit` reads the same source and returns findings without writing files. Both run with Node alone: no PHP, WordPress installation, database, WP-CLI or network requests. Extraction is independent of `files`, `.distignore` and Git `export-ignore` distribution rules."},"302":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#wordpress-internationalization","content":"Config-free source directories must have plugin/theme headers. The actual main file is discovered using the package metadata reader, including plugins whose main filename differs from their slug. The selected domain defaults to `Text Domain`, then the directory name. Calls with omitted domains belong to WordPress's `default` domain; block metadata without a domain inherits the package domain. `--all-domains` explicitly combines domains. A block's explicit `textdomain` always controls its messages."},"303":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#wordpress-internationalization","content":"`--package` selects one configured package and permits extraction-specific overrides; `--all` requires configuration and selects enabled WordPress packages only. Source directories, package selectors and `--all` are mutually exclusive. Config-free directory mode reads only its explicit options and metadata; it does not import repository config implicitly. A disabled package fails when explicitly selected. Configuration uses `i18n` at the root and on package entries, including an exact `glob` for an individual package, following the existing group model. Package entries remain non-overlapping; there is no additional package manifest configuration source."},"304":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#wordpress-internationalization","content":"Precedence is defaults, root configuration, package group/individual entry, then explicit command options. Scalars override, lists replace inherited lists, and gettext headers merge by key, case-insensitively, preserving intentionally empty values. A header set to `false` is removed, like `'Project-Id-Version': false` to keep the plugin version out of the POT so that it changes only when strings change; a later layer can set it again. `Content-Type` can't be removed, since it declares the UTF-8 charset. Options include `enabled`, `domain`, `allDomains`, `include`, `exclude`, `destination`, `headers`, `fileComment`, `references`, `strict`, `legacyGlobals` and audit `suppress`. `fileComment` adds static text above the POT header; each line is emitted as a gettext comment, and no copyright year is generated. The CLI accepts the same setting with `--file-comment`. Repeat `--include` or `--exclude` for multiple globs; these use case-sensitive minimatch semantics with `/` paths, `**`, brace expansion and dotfile matching, without gitignore negation or comma splitting. Default excludes cover dependency directories, dot directories, tests, minified files and generated translations. Source/build exclusions are a repository choice; this repository excludes `build`, `tools` and isolated vendors in its config."},"305":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#wordpress-internationalization","content":"The default output is `<Domain Path>/<text-domain>.pot`, or `languages/<text-domain>.pot`. A leading `/` in `Domain Path` is package-relative, and configured destinations cannot escape the package. `--destination` changes the package-relative directory. An explicit `--output <file.pot>` resolves against the command's working directory and requires one package. All multi-package destinations are validated before writing; each completed package replaces its file atomically, independently of other packages. `--check` compares bytes without writing and fails if the POT is absent or stale. Source references are preserved by default; `--no-references` omits them. `POT-Creation-Date` and `PO-Revision-Date` default to empty values; no current date or year is inserted. Name/version headers come from metadata, and `--header NAME=VALUE` overrides any default, including with an empty value, and `--header NAME` removes the header. The POT lists the plugin or theme header strings first, then the strings of PHP, JavaScript, `block.json` and other metadata files in source order, so related strings stay together for translators. Each entry has its extracted comments, references and flags before the strings, in the usual gettext layout. The default headers come in a fixed order, and custom headers follow them. No generator header or generated copyright comment is added; set `fileComment` for a static one."},"306":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#supported-extraction-syntax","content":"PHP extraction uses `php-parser` in PHP 8.3 mode, tested with attributes, enums, match expressions, promoted properties and readonly classes. Supported WordPress functions are `__`, `_e`, `_x`, `_ex`, `_n`, `_nx`, `_n_noop`, `_nx_noop`, `esc_attr__`, `esc_attr_e`, `esc_attr_x`, `esc_html__`, `esc_html_e` and `esc_html_x`. Global and explicitly global calls, namespace fallback and explicit function imports/aliases are supported. Known file-local declarations and unrelated namespaced imports are excluded; methods and static methods are never translation calls. Named arguments resolve by PHP parameter name. Literal strings, static concatenation and non-interpolated heredoc/nowdoc preserve decoded escapes, Unicode and newlines. Counts may be dynamic; IDs, contexts and selected domains must be static."},"307":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#supported-extraction-syntax","content":"JavaScript extraction uses Babel's parser and lexical scope traversal, without transforms or project Babel config. JS, JSX, TS, TSX, MJS, CJS, MTS and CTS are supported. Named imports/aliases and namespace imports from `@wordpress/i18n`, unshadowed `wp.i18n` calls and unbound canonical legacy `__`, `_x`, `_n` and `_nx` calls extract strings. `--no-legacy-globals` requires imported or `wp.i18n` bindings. Locally shadowed/reassigned imports are excluded. Static literals, templates without substitutions, concatenation and TypeScript assertions are supported. Destructured CommonJS imports, transpiled `(0, i18n.__)` forms and arbitrary minified bundles are outside the supported syntax; scan source and use explicit source/build mapping for compiled JSON."},"308":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#supported-extraction-syntax","content":"A `translators:` comment attaches to the first translation call whose containing expression or nearest statement immediately follows it. Whitespace, parsed comments and ternary branch punctuation may intervene, but unrelated statements or argument expressions may not. Comments before outer wrappers reach nested translation calls; a comment is consumed once, so unrelated sibling calls do not inherit it. Multiline comment content is preserved. WordPress plugin/theme header labels serialize as extracted comments. Bundled metadata tables follow WordPress 6.8 block fields and theme fields, including styles, variations, presets, template names and nested block settings. Their upstream data license is GPL-2.0-or-later; tables are maintained intentionally with fixtures and never fetched during generation."},"309":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#supported-extraction-syntax","content":"Invalid syntax, unreadable selected files, selected source symlinks, empty/reserved message IDs, conflicting plural definitions and output errors fail generation. Unsupported dynamic arguments warn and are fatal with `--strict`. Audit adds `placeholder-comment`, `unordered-placeholders`, `plural-placeholders`, `empty-message` and `conflicting-comments` findings; suppress these explicitly by code where justified. Placeholders support `%%`, positional indexes, numeric width/precision, padding/flags and PHP/JS sprintf conversion types. Audit checks extraction and message quality, not every untranslated user-facing string. Audit fails on errors; strict audit also fails on warnings. Failed checks return exit code `2`; unexpected I/O/option-loading errors follow the existing CLI error handling. `--json`, GitHub annotations, summaries and step outputs use the existing CLI reporting facilities."},"310":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#compile-translated-catalogs","content":"Runtime formats require a translated PO, not an untranslated POT. PO updating, fuzzy matching and automatic translation are not provided. Compilation validates `Language` and `Plural-Forms`, excludes fuzzy, obsolete and untranslated entries, preserves contexts and plural forms, and never changes the input PO. An incompletely translated plural entry is excluded as a unit. Gettext parsing handles source encoding; runtime outputs use UTF-8. Each output is replaced atomically; JSON outputs are independent writes, and obsolete output files are not deleted automatically."},"311":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#compile-translated-catalogs","content":"MO and `.l10n.php` default beside the PO, with `.mo` and `.l10n.php` extensions; `--output` selects an explicit file relative to cwd. WordPress PHP catalogs require WordPress 6.5 or later. JSON requires an explicit domain; `--output` is a directory, defaulting to the PO directory. `--check` compares proposed bytes without writing and exits `2` on absent/stale outputs. Compiler commands are primitives and do not select configured packages."},"312":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#compile-translated-catalogs","content":"A script map is a JSON object from normalized package-relative source references to arrays of normalized runtime script paths. Many source files can feed one bundle, and one source can feed several scripts. An explicit map selects only listed references; an empty target array excludes that source. Without a map, JS/MJS/CJS references use identity paths, while JSX/TypeScript references require mapping. PHP-only references do not enter JSON catalogs."},"313":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#compile-translated-catalogs","content":"JSON filenames are `<domain>-<Language>-<md5(runtime-path)>.json`; the WordPress `default` domain uses `<Language>-<md5(runtime-path)>.json`. WordPress's `.min.js` normalization applies consistently to grouping, hashing and the JSON `source`. Jed keys preserve context through `\\u0004`, plural arrays contain all translated forms, and revision dates come from the PO metadata. `.l10n.php` writes safely escaped data arrays with NUL-separated plural translations; catalog text is never treated as executable PHP."},"314":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/internationalization#compile-translated-catalogs","content":"The package can be installed and used in any WordPress plugin or theme project; repository package selection is optional, and directory mode works without a wp-release config."},"315":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","type":"page","content":"Library API","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library"},"316":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#exit-codes","content":"Exit codes"},"317":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#library","content":"Library"},"318":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"Logging"},"319":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#exit-codes","content":"| Code | Meaning                               |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `0`  | Success, dry run, no changes, or help |","table":"table-0"},"320":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#exit-codes","content":"| Code | Meaning                                                 |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `1`  | Unexpected failure, such as a failed svn or git command |","table":"table-0"},"321":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#exit-codes","content":"| Code | Meaning                                                                                                                                          |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `2`  | Invalid options, failed release checks, an existing SVN tag, a Git tag that exists with another release, or files that don't match the checksums |","table":"table-0"},"322":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#exit-codes","content":"| Code | Meaning                                                   |\n| ---- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `3`  | Missing SVN credentials, or credentials that SVN rejected |","table":"table-0"},"323":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#library","content":"`resolveDeployOptions()` applies the defaults and throws a `ValidationError` (with `exitCode` `2`) for invalid combinations. `SvnDeploy` exposes each step (`prepare()`, `checkVersions()`, `checkout()`, `sync()`, `commit()` and `cleanup()`) separately so CI wrappers can run and report them one by one; `run()` runs all of them, or nothing when there's nothing to write. `getPlan()` lists the planned SVN changes. The `Svn` class wraps the svn command line, and takes the command runner as a dependency so that tests can replace it."},"324":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#library","content":"Git deploys:"},"325":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#library","content":"`resolveGitDeployOptions()` throws a `ValidationError` without a repository URL or with an invalid branch or tag. `GitDeploy` exposes `createTree()`, `fetchPrevious()`, `createRelease()`, `isOutdated()`, `checkTag()`, `getRefsToPush()` and `push()` separately, like `SvnDeploy`, and takes a git command runner from `createGitRunner()` as a dependency. The result has `outdated` when the branch wasn't moved for older source history, the refs to write in `planned` and the refs that were pushed in `pushed`, which is empty in a dry run. `redactUrls()` hides the credentials in URLs."},"326":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#library","content":"Changeset releases:"},"327":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#library","content":"`ChangesetRelease` runs the `changeset-*` commands: `add()`, `getStatus()`, `version()` and `tag()`, plus `getPlan()`, `getPackages()` and `getConfiguredPackages()`. Each call reads the configuration, packages and pending changesets again, so one instance can version and then tag. `getConfiguredPackages()` returns package directories with their resolved release group metadata; `formatTag()` and `formatTemplate()` apply the same placeholders as the release workflows. It takes a git runner from `createGitRunner()` as `git`, so that tests can replace it. Without a config file, it detects a single root project. Its optional `singleProject` settings accept `versions` (the existing version descriptors), `versionFrom` (the same selector as `--version-from`), `baseBranch`, `remote`, `changesets`, `readmeChangelog` and `changelogInsertAfter`; `tagFormat` remains a repository option. The functions `addChangeset()`, `getChangesetStatus()`, `versionChangesets()`, `tagChangesets()`, `getChangesetReleasePlan()`, `getChangesetPackages()` and `getConfiguredPackages()` do the same with the default runners, in one call."},"328":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"`Logger` filters messages by level and passes them to a writer, like the transports of other Node.js loggers. The writer decides how messages look:"},"329":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"`TextWriter` prints plain text to `stdout` and `stderr`. It's the default."},"330":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"`GitHubActionsWriter` prints workflow commands, so that notices, warnings and errors with a file are annotations, and appends summaries to `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`. It's the CLI's `github` log format, and the GitHub Actions pass it to their logger. Its `workspace` and `summaryPath` options default to `GITHUB_WORKSPACE` and `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`."},"331":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"A writer implements `LogWriter` with `write()`, `startGroup()`, `endGroup()` and `summary()`, so other CI systems can have their own format. `summary()` takes a function that builds the Markdown, which only writers with a summary call."},"332":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"A `ValidationError` can list `problems`, each with a `message` and an optional `file`, `line` and `title`. `logError( logger, error, title )` logs each problem as an error on its file and line and lists them in the summary under the title, or logs the message when there are none. `VersionMismatchError` and `version-check` report the version mismatches this way."},"333":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"ZIP files and metadata:"},"334":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"Checksums:"},"335":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"`loadFileRules()` reads the ignore file and `--exclude` patterns into a gitignore matcher, optionally adds Git `export-ignore` paths, and `selectFiles()` lists the files it doesn't ignore. `compareChecksums()` compares two hash maps without reading any files, and `parseChecksums()` validates manifest JSON. `extractZip()` extracts a ZIP after the same checks as `deploy-svn --from-zip`."},"336":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"Translations:"},"337":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/library","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/library#logging","content":"Parsers return messages and structured diagnostics with stable codes, severity and source-relative locations. Catalog messages retain IDs, contexts, plurals, translations, translator comments, flags and structured references. `aggregateCatalog()` selects/merges a domain, unions references/comments and diagnoses plural conflicts. `resolveI18nPackages()` is the separate config adapter; extraction and serialization primitives know nothing about the repository. Library functions do not log, parse process arguments or set exit codes. Extraction results report unique files consulted, including the main plugin/theme file used for headers and header strings even when source globs exclude it. `parseTranslatedPo()`, `compileMo()`, `compileScriptTranslations()`, `compilePhpTranslations()` and `compileTranslations()` expose the same typed operations to library callers."},"338":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"page","content":"Packaging and integrity","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Js","Wp release","Latest"],"tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging"},"339":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-0","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#build-a-directory","content":"Build a directory"},"340":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-1","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"ZIP"},"341":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-2","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signing-targets-for-builds-and-zips","content":"Signing targets for builds and ZIPs"},"342":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-3","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#checksums","content":"Checksums"},"343":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-4","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#filenames-embedding-and-signing","content":"Filenames, embedding and signing"},"344":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-5","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#formats","content":"Formats"},"345":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-6","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#configuration-and-custom-codecs","content":"Configuration and custom codecs"},"346":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-7","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"Manifest inside the plugin"},"347":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-8","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"Verify"},"348":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-9","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"Sign"},"349":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-10","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"Signature formats"},"350":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-11","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#version-check","content":"Version check"},"351":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-12","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#build-a-directory","content":"`build` and `zip` use the same internal helper to load file rules, exclude their outputs, select release files and reject an empty selection. `build` copies the selected files directly into `--output <dir>`, without a slug wrapper or ZIP. Checksums and manifest signatures are optional. It supports the shared file selection flags above and `--package <name>` with `--config <path>` for configured package rules. A configured package cannot be combined with a source directory or file selection flags."},"352":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-13","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#build-a-directory","content":"The destination is required and automatically excluded when it is inside the source. It cannot be the source directory or an ancestor, including through symlinked parents. Existing destinations require `--force`, which replaces the directory and removes stale files; existing files and symbolic links are rejected. Files are copied to a temporary directory before the existing build is replaced. Selected symbolic links are rejected as with ZIP packaging. `--json` returns `outputDir`, `files` and, for configured builds, `package`; it also returns `checksumsPath` when a manifest is generated, and `signatures` and `publicKey` when signing is requested. `--github-output` exposes these results too."},"353":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-14","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#build-a-directory","content":"`--checksums` writes `<output-dir>.checksums.json` beside the directory and hashes the copied build files. `--sign-checksums` generates and signs that manifest, implying `--checksums`. Use `--slug` and `--version` to override its metadata. The manifest and its signatures are excluded from the source selection, so rebuilding with `--force` does not package previous sidecars. Existing manifests and requested signature files also require `--force`; signing keys, formats and signature destinations are validated before creating or replacing the build. `--sign` is unsupported because a directory has no single file to sign."},"354":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-15","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#build-a-directory","content":"In the Deploy workflow, a directory build can be deployed with `wp-release deploy-svn dist/code-prettify --slug code-prettify --trunk --svn-tag \"$GITHUB_REF_NAME\" --no-distignore`. Keep `--from-zip` when the SVN deployment must match the attached ZIP exactly. Run dependency installation and compilation before `build`; it only selects and copies files."},"355":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-16","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"The `wp-release-zip` GitHub Action runs this command."},"356":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-17","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`zip` writes `{slug}.{version}.zip` to the current directory, or to `--output <file>`, with the selected files in a `{slug}/` root folder:"},"357":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-18","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`--root-folder <name>` changes the root folder and `--no-root-folder` puts the files at the root of the ZIP."},"358":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-19","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`--filename-format` changes the name, like `--filename-format {slug}` for `my-plugin.zip`."},"359":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-20","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"Entries are sorted and use a fixed timestamp and file mode. Built-in metadata is deterministic except for embedded minisign signatures, which include their signing timestamp; custom codec callbacks control their own encoded bytes."},"360":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-21","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`--checksums` also writes the checksums manifest of the archived files next to the ZIP, with `.checksums.json` instead of `.zip`."},"361":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-22","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`--sign` signs only the ZIP with `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY`. `--sign-checksums` generates and signs only the checksums manifest, implying `--checksums`. Use both flags to sign both files. External signatures are written alongside their files; embedded manifest signatures are written inside the package. The default format is WordPress `.sig`, unless integrity configuration selects another format."},"362":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-23","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`--sign-format` selects formats for requested signatures, such as `--sign-format wordpress,minisign,ssh`. It requires `--sign` or `--sign-checksums` and never enables signing by itself. Signing keys, formats and existing signature destinations are validated before writing the ZIP or manifest. `--force` also overwrites requested signatures. Job summaries include signatures and public keys; JSON results include `signatures` and `publicKey`."},"363":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-24","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"An existing ZIP or manifest isn't overwritten without `--force`. A ZIP or manifest written inside the source directory is never archived."},"364":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-25","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"`--package <name>` zips a package of the configured packages, by identifier or path under the configured root like `plugins/alpha`, instead of a source directory: its directory with the `files` config of its package group, the same files that its deploys select. The slug defaults to the directory name and the version to the `Version` header, and `--slug` and `--version` still set them. Any package of the config works, with or without a version or deploy targets. To zip what a deploy just released, pass the `package` and `version` of the `deploy-git --release-ref` result, in the same job and from the same build, so the ZIP has exactly the files of the release tag in the release repository. It can't be combined with `<source-dir>` or the file selection options. The `--json` result has the `package`, `slug` and `version`. `zipPackage()` builds the same ZIP from a script, with `getPackageFiles()` for the directory and the files of a package."},"365":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-26","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"To embed a signed manifest with a stable filename, use:"},"366":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-27","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#zip","content":"See Checksums for filenames, placement, formats and configuration."},"367":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-28","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signing-targets-for-builds-and-zips","content":"| Flag          | `zip`                                         | `build`                                             |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--checksums` | Generate an unsigned manifest beside the ZIP. | Generate an unsigned manifest beside the directory. |","table":"table-0"},"368":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-29","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signing-targets-for-builds-and-zips","content":"| Flag     | `zip`              | `build`      |\n| -------- | ------------------ | ------------ |\n| `--sign` | Sign only the ZIP. | Unsupported. |","table":"table-0"},"369":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-30","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signing-targets-for-builds-and-zips","content":"| Flag               | `zip`                           | `build`                         |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `--sign-checksums` | Generate and sign the manifest. | Generate and sign the manifest. |","table":"table-0"},"370":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-31","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signing-targets-for-builds-and-zips","content":"| Flag            | `zip`                                               | `build`                            |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `--sign-format` | Choose formats for explicitly requested signatures. | Same; requires `--sign-checksums`. |","table":"table-0"},"371":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-32","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signing-targets-for-builds-and-zips","content":"These flags deliberately replace the former implicit signing behavior: `zip --checksums --sign` now leaves the manifest unsigned, and `--sign-format` alone is rejected. Add `--sign-checksums` to sign a manifest and `--sign` to sign a ZIP. The standalone `checksums` and `sign` commands remain available for workflows that write and sign a manifest inside the source before packaging it."},"372":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-33","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#checksums","content":"The `wp-release-checksums` GitHub Action runs this command."},"373":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-34","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#checksums","content":"`checksums` writes a manifest with the MD5 and SHA-256 hash of every selected file, in the format of the WordPress.org plugin checksums API (`https://downloads.wordpress.org/plugin-checksums/{slug}/{version}.json`). Paths are relative to the plugin or theme folder, sorted, and use forward slashes:"},"374":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-35","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#checksums","content":"Themes use a `theme` key instead of `plugin`, and the version is left out when it's unknown. The manifest is named `{slug}.{version}.checksums.json` after `--filename-format`, the same name as the ZIP with `.checksums.json` instead of `.zip`, so the two are published side by side; `--output <file>` writes it elsewhere. A manifest written inside `<source-dir>` is never listed in itself."},"375":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-36","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#checksums","content":"Because the manifest lists the files and not the ZIP, it stays valid for any copy of the same files: the GitHub release ZIP, the ZIP that WordPress.org builds from SVN, or an installed plugin on a site."},"376":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-37","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#filenames-embedding-and-signing","content":"`--checksums-file <file>` enables checksums and sets a relative filename independently of the ZIP name. Without `--embed-checksums`, it resolves against the ZIP output directory; with embedding, it resolves against the package root inside the ZIP (the archive root with `--no-root-folder`). Nested paths are supported, but absolute paths, backslashes, drive letters, control characters and dot segments are rejected. Embedding emits no external manifest and never modifies the source directory. Embedded minisign signatures contain a signing timestamp, so ZIP bytes can differ across signing times; unsigned metadata and WordPress/SSH signatures remain deterministic. Selected source files must not collide with generated manifest or signature entries."},"377":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-38","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#filenames-embedding-and-signing","content":"`--sign-checksums` enables checksums and signs only the manifest, before embedding when requested. It reads `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY` and uses `--sign-format wordpress,minisign,ssh` to select formats; each signature travels with the manifest. `--sign-format` selects formats for explicitly requested signing targets and never enables signing. `--sign` signs only the final ZIP; `--sign-checksums` controls the manifest in either placement mode. Use both flags to sign both targets. `--force` permits replacing external output files and signatures. `--json` reports external manifests in `checksumsPath`, embedded package-relative paths in `checksumsEmbedded`, and manifest signatures in `checksumsSignatures`."},"378":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-39","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#filenames-embedding-and-signing","content":"The default WordPress filename remains the ZIP basename with `.checksums.json` instead of `.zip`, in either placement mode. Choose `--checksums-file checksums.json` for a stable name. The version remains in the JSON content."},"379":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-40","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#formats","content":"`--checksums-format` chooses the manifest format. `wordpress-json` (default) uses the WordPress-compatible structure shown above. `sha256sum` uses GNU-compatible SHA-256 lines and escaping, with `SHA256SUMS` as its default filename. It carries no package identity or version, and standard `sha256sum --check` checks listed files without rejecting additional files; `wp-release verify` also checks for extra files."},"380":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-41","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#formats","content":"`--checksums-algorithms md5,sha256` selects JSON digest fields; lists replace configured lists. Supported algorithms are MD5 (for compatibility) and SHA-256. The `sha256sum` codec requires SHA-256. The standalone `checksums` command also accepts `--checksums-format`, keeping `--output` relative to the working directory."},"381":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-42","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#configuration-and-custom-codecs","content":"The optional top-level `integrity` configuration maps to these flags. Explicit CLI values override configuration; `--no-embed-checksums` and `--no-sign-checksums` disable their configured behaviors. Existing release configurations retain their `packages` array; use `packages: []` for a configuration containing only integrity settings."},"382":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-43","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#configuration-and-custom-codecs","content":"Use `codec: 'sha256sum'`, `algorithms: ['sha256']`, `file: 'SHA256SUMS'` and `sign: { formats: ['minisign'] }` for the GNU setup. Custom codecs provide `id`, a default `filename`, and `encode`/`decode` callbacks, which may be asynchronous. Generation validates the round trip before writing artifacts: codecs must preserve all selected file paths, represented digests and any represented package identity. They receive and return the existing WordPress-compatible `ChecksumsManifest` model (`plugin` or `theme`, optional `version`, and a `files` map of relative paths to digest fields), keeping format customization separate from selection, hashing and signing."},"383":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-44","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#configuration-and-custom-codecs","content":"`zip` and `checksums` read the caller's release configuration. For verification of custom codecs or configured filenames, pass `--config wp-release.config.mjs` explicitly. See Verify."},"384":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-45","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"For a manifest that is also published as a separate release asset or deployed directly from a source directory, write and sign it in that directory before packaging. Use `zip --embed-checksums` as described above when only the archive should contain the generated metadata. The source-directory workflow uses the same manifest for installed copies and release assets:"},"385":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-46","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"Write the manifest into the directory that is zipped and deployed. The `checksums` command leaves its output manifest and its signatures (`<name>.sig`, `<name>.minisig` and `<name>.sshsig`) out of the hashes, so running it again with the same output path and `--force` gives the same manifest. `verify` checks matching copies as described below. Sign it last, since the signature covers the exact bytes of the manifest."},"386":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-47","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"`zip` and `deploy-svn` carry the pair like any other file. Don't use `zip --checksums` here, since it would write a second manifest that lists the first one. Attach `build/checksums.json` and `build/checksums.json.sig` to the GitHub release as they are."},"387":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-48","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"The signature covers file hashes and not a ZIP, so it stays valid for the ZIP that WordPress.org builds from SVN. Check what WordPress.org serves after a deploy, with the manifest and public key from your build:"},"388":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-49","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"The manifest can have any name and location, for example `--output build/includes/signatures/checksums.json`, and missing folders are created. Tell `verify` where the shipped copy is with `--embedded <path>`, like `--embedded includes/signatures/checksums.json`. Both the `--checksums` file and its signature must then exist, both copies must be shipped, and they're skipped when comparing files and must be identical to the originals. The manifest can't list the embedded path. Without `--embedded`, `verify` looks for the manifest name at the root, and skips a file only when it exists, the matching original exists and the manifest doesn't list it. Otherwise it's checked like any other file, so a listed file that has the name of the manifest can't be deleted unnoticed, and a shipped `.sig` without an original is reported as not in the checksums."},"389":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-50","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#manifest-inside-the-plugin","content":"The public key has to come from somewhere other than the plugin that it checks, because someone who can overwrite the plugin files can also replace a key and a signature inside them. A copy of the manifest also doesn't prevent a downgrade to an older release that was signed too, so the installing side has to enforce a minimum version."},"390":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-51","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"`verify` checks a directory against a manifest from `checksums` or from the WordPress.org checksums API, and reports every changed, missing and extra file. It exits with `2` unless all files match:"},"391":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-52","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"A file matches when its SHA-256 equals the manifest. Manifests without SHA-256 fall back to MD5, and a hash can also be a list of accepted values."},"392":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-53","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"`verify` doesn't read `.distignore`, so that an ignore file inside the checked directory can't hide added files. `--exclude <patterns>` skips paths in both the directory and the manifest, for files that aren't part of the release, such as caches."},"393":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-54","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"Manifest paths outside the package folder (absolute or with `..`) are rejected."},"394":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-55","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"`--zip <file|url>` checks the files of a ZIP instead of a directory. The ZIP can be a URL, such as the one that WordPress.org builds for a release, and its single root folder is found automatically (or set `--root-folder`). The ZIP is extracted to a temporary directory after the same safety checks as `deploy-svn --from-zip`."},"395":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-56","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"The version in the manifest must match the `Version` header of the checked files, so that an older release with its own valid manifest can't pass as a newer one."},"396":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-57","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"A manifest can't list itself. A copy of the manifest and of its signature inside the checked files, at the root with the same names as the `--checksums` file or at the path of `--embedded`, is skipped when comparing files, but it must be identical to the `--checksums` file and its signature. The signature is the `.sig` file, or the file of `--signature-format`. A path that the manifest lists is never skipped, and a file that isn't a matching copy is reported as not in the checksums. See Manifest inside the plugin."},"397":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-58","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"A checksums manifest proves only that the files match the manifest. With `--public-key <key>`, `verify` first checks `<checksums>.sig` against the Ed25519 public key and fails with exit code `2` before comparing any file when the signature is missing or invalid, so that the manifest itself can be trusted. `--signature-format minisign` or `ssh` checks `<checksums>.minisig` or `<checksums>.sshsig` instead. The key can be the base64 key, the minisign public key or the `ssh-ed25519` public key, since they're forms of the same key. See Sign."},"398":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-59","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#verify","content":"Verification never executes configuration from inside a downloaded package. Without `--checksums`, `verify` reads the embedded filename selected by `--checksums-file`, explicit configuration, or the codec default. For a versioned embedded filename, pass that filename explicitly. With a public key, signatures are checked before decoding; the selected signature format must exist (WordPress `.sig` by default; pass `--signature-format minisign` or `ssh` for those formats), and all other present supported signature formats are also authenticated when verifying the embedded manifest. Without a public key, checksums detect changes but do not authenticate the publisher. Trusted public keys must come from outside the package."},"399":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-60","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"The `wp-release-sign` GitHub Action runs this command."},"400":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-61","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"`sign` writes a detached signature next to each file, `<file>.sig`, so that downloads and installed copies can be trusted. Sign both the ZIP and the checksums manifest: the ZIP signature covers installs and updates, and the manifest signature covers the contents after unpacking and copies that weren't built by you."},"401":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-62","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"The signature is the one WordPress core's `verify_file_signature()` checks: a base64 Ed25519 signature of the raw SHA-384 digest of the file. The tests check it against `sodium_crypto_sign_verify_detached()` in PHP. A `.sig` file can hold several signatures, one per line, and a file is valid when any of them match, which allows a key rotation window."},"402":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-63","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"The key is read from the `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY` environment variable only, as a base64 32-byte Ed25519 seed or a 64-byte libsodium secret key, the formats that `sodium_crypto_sign_seed_keypair()` and the update-pilot-server package signing key take. There's no flag for it, since it would show in the process list, and it's never logged."},"403":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-64","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"`--verbose` prints the public key of the signing key in the form of each written format. `--json` returns it as `publicKey`, and in every form as `publicKeys`. Pass it to `verify --public-key` and to the plugin that checks the signatures."},"404":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-65","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"An existing signature file isn't overwritten without `--force`."},"405":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-66","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#sign","content":"`zip --sign` signs only the ZIP; add `--sign-checksums` to sign its manifest too. `signFiles()` signs files from a script."},"406":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-67","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"The same key can also sign in formats that tools outside WordPress check, for people who download a release by hand or verify it in other pipelines. Pick them with `--format`, comma-separated or repeated; the default is `wordpress`:"},"407":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-68","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"| Format      | File         | Checked by                                                                        |\n| ----------- | ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wordpress` | `<file>.sig` | WordPress core's `verify_file_signature()`, update-client and `wp-release verify` |","table":"table-1"},"408":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-69","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"| Format     | File             | Checked by                                                                    |\n| ---------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `minisign` | `<file>.minisig` | minisign and compatible tools: `minisign -Vm <file> -P <minisign public key>` |","table":"table-1"},"409":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-70","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"| Format | File            | Checked by                                                                                                      |\n| ------ | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ssh`  | `<file>.sshsig` | OpenSSH 8.1 or later: `ssh-keygen -Y verify -f allowed_signers -I <identity> -n file -s <file>.sshsig < <file>` |","table":"table-1"},"410":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-71","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"`minisign` signs the BLAKE2b-512 digest of the file, like `minisign -S` does by default, and adds a trusted comment with the signing time and file name that a second signature covers. minisign generates a random key ID for its keys, but the key here is a plain Ed25519 seed, so wp-release derives the key ID from the public key. `wp-release verify` doesn't compare key IDs, so signatures of keys that minisign generated verify too."},"411":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-72","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"`ssh` is the armored `SSHSIG` format that `ssh-keygen -Y sign` writes, with a SHA-512 digest and the `file` namespace, so that the signature can't pass as one for commits or another namespace. It's written to `.sshsig` instead of the `.sig` of `ssh-keygen`, which the `wordpress` format uses. Put the SSH public key in an `allowed_signers` file as `<identity> ssh-ed25519 AAAA…`."},"412":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-73","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"All formats are Ed25519 signatures by the same key, so one key and one secret cover all of them. The tests check each format with `minisign`, `ssh-keygen` and libsodium, and check the signatures that `minisign` and `ssh-keygen` write with `wp-release verify`."},"413":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-74","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#signature-formats","content":"Generate a key pair with `wp-release keygen`. It prints the private seed once as `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY=…` and the matching public key, also as a minisign public key and an `ssh-ed25519` public key. Generate keys locally and store the private seed in your secret manager or CI secrets. Distribute the public key to verifiers. `keygen --github-output` is rejected to keep the private seed out of workflow outputs; regular and `--json` output both include the seed."},"414":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-75","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#version-check","content":"`version-check` compares the versions that have to agree before a release, and reports every mismatch with exit code `2`:"},"415":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-76","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#version-check","content":"The expected version is `--version`, or the `Version` header."},"416":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-77","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#version-check","content":"The readme `Stable tag` must match for plugins; `--no-stable-tag` skips it, and themes have no Stable tag."},"417":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-78","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#version-check","content":"`--constant NAME` (repeatable) checks `define( 'NAME', '1.2.0' )` or `const NAME = '1.2.0'` in every PHP file outside `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot directories. A constant that isn't defined is a mismatch."},"418":{"id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging-79","page_id":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/js/wp-release/latest/packaging#version-check","content":"`--git-tag` defaults to `GITHUB_REF` when it's a tag. `--git-tag-format` (default `{version}`) can have a prefix or the slug, like `v{version}` or `{slug}-{version}`; a leading `v` in the version is ignored, like for SVN tags."}},"count":418},"sorting":{"language":"english","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{"content":{"docs":{"1":246,"2":247,"3":335,"4":334,"5":316,"6":324,"7":336,"8":350,"9":339,"10":286,"11":228,"12":280,"13":378,"14":147,"15":146,"16":144,"17":145,"18":143,"19":142,"20":148,"21":281,"22":208,"23":206,"24":210,"25":207,"26":204,"27":205,"28":209,"29":238,"30":394,"31":235,"32":76,"33":387,"34":285,"35":353,"36":11,"37":18,"38":201,"39":202,"40":203,"41":268,"42":3,"43":1,"44":0,"45":15,"46":2,"47":355,"48":408,"49":231,"50":409,"51":296,"52":314,"53":262,"54":263,"55":278,"56":403,"57":349,"58":53,"59":341,"60":233,"61":392,"62":340,"63":232,"64":390,"65":375,"66":287,"67":49,"68":226,"69":333,"70":348,"71":50,"72":250,"73":251,"74":292,"75":98,"76":104,"77":102,"78":109,"79":112,"80":96,"81":99,"82":100,"83":95,"84":93,"85":94,"86":103,"87":101,"88":107,"89":97,"90":105,"91":110,"92":108,"93":106,"94":111,"95":327,"96":297,"97":326,"98":407,"99":164,"100":163,"101":320,"102":277,"103":74,"104":71,"105":275,"106":117,"107":118,"108":122,"109":121,"110":119,"111":123,"112":120,"113":116,"114":114,"115":113,"116":115,"117":223,"118":397,"119":256,"120":254,"121":211,"122":217,"123":215,"124":218,"125":216,"126":212,"127":213,"128":214,"129":259,"130":312,"131":282,"132":322,"133":44,"134":86,"135":87,"136":84,"137":88,"138":81,"139":82,"140":83,"141":85,"142":365,"143":126,"144":133,"145":141,"146":127,"147":124,"148":138,"149":130,"150":131,"151":137,"152":136,"153":125,"154":134,"155":132,"156":140,"157":135,"158":129,"159":128,"160":139,"161":253,"162":385,"163":307,"164":48,"165":199,"166":200,"167":198,"168":197,"169":43,"170":352,"171":41,"172":271,"173":270,"174":269,"175":267,"176":343,"177":57,"178":158,"179":154,"180":149,"181":150,"182":157,"183":395,"184":294,"185":367,"186":406,"187":376,"188":359,"189":240,"190":402,"191":29,"192":266,"193":398,"194":33,"195":40,"196":323,"197":38,"198":229,"199":55,"200":152,"201":155,"202":295,"203":379,"204":371,"205":404,"206":405,"207":383,"208":374,"209":391,"210":27,"211":54,"212":168,"213":166,"214":167,"215":165,"216":56,"217":9,"218":16,"219":14,"220":5,"221":17,"222":13,"223":10,"224":8,"225":222,"226":315,"227":272,"228":273,"229":239,"230":382,"231":47,"232":69,"233":61,"234":361,"235":347,"236":58,"237":414,"238":12,"239":19,"240":264,"241":360,"242":370,"243":20,"244":7,"245":6,"246":4,"247":265,"248":351,"249":175,"250":179,"251":176,"252":173,"253":177,"254":172,"255":186,"256":180,"257":188,"258":182,"259":187,"260":178,"261":184,"262":174,"263":183,"264":191,"265":362,"266":189,"267":193,"268":190,"269":192,"270":195,"271":170,"272":185,"273":194,"274":171,"275":196,"276":169,"277":181,"278":413,"279":243,"280":299,"281":337,"282":293,"283":45,"284":346,"285":412,"286":313,"287":369,"288":252,"289":244,"290":261,"291":318,"292":290,"293":410,"294":305,"295":274,"296":255,"297":300,"298":411,"299":338,"300":257,"301":60,"302":258,"303":32,"304":325,"305":354,"306":321,"307":302,"308":219,"309":301,"310":328,"311":311,"312":227,"313":303,"314":368,"315":306,"316":279,"317":304,"318":308,"319":92,"320":91,"321":89,"322":90,"323":65,"324":291,"325":66,"326":245,"327":51,"328":63,"329":70,"330":59,"331":230,"332":220,"333":417,"334":249,"335":62,"336":393,"337":319,"338":317,"339":242,"340":416,"341":332,"342":248,"343":283,"344":288,"345":260,"346":309,"347":400,"348":330,"349":331,"350":401,"351":46,"352":357,"353":25,"354":298,"355":345,"356":80,"357":34,"358":28,"359":276,"360":24,"361":37,"362":36,"363":237,"364":31,"365":389,"366":329,"367":156,"368":159,"369":151,"370":153,"371":388,"372":342,"373":52,"374":386,"375":241,"376":22,"377":35,"378":356,"379":23,"380":21,"381":366,"382":396,"383":78,"384":284,"385":415,"386":79,"387":380,"388":364,"389":372,"390":72,"391":224,"392":73,"393":310,"394":42,"395":384,"396":225,"397":221,"398":399,"399":344,"400":67,"401":381,"402":363,"403":39,"404":236,"405":77,"406":377,"407":160,"408":161,"409":162,"410":64,"411":68,"412":234,"413":289,"414":75,"415":358,"416":373,"417":26,"418":30},"orderedDocs":[[44,"**`--tag-format <format>`** replaces the `tagFormat` of the config for one command, for a workflow or job that names its tags differently, like `--tag-format '{name}@{version}'`. Every command that creates or reads the same tags needs the same format: `changeset-tag`, which creates them and finds the previous tag for the release notes, `changeset-version --prerelease`, which numbers pre-releases from them, and `deploy-git` and `deploy-svn --release-ref`, which read the package and version of a tag."],[43,"**`releaseTitle`** titles the release, like the `title` that `changeset-tag` reports for creating a GitHub Release. The default is `name@version`, like `plugins/alpha@1.2.0`, without the prefix for a package at the repository root, which is titled `1.2.0` like its tag. Like `tagFormat`, it supports `{name}`, `{path}`, `{version}` and `{tag}` placeholders: `releaseTitle: '{version} (beta)'`, or a callback that receives `{ package, version, tag }` like the release notes callback. A template without a placeholder fails at load time, since it would name every release alike. `changeset-tag --release-title <template>` replaces the `releaseTitle` of the config for one command, like `--tag-format` replaces `tagFormat`."],[46,"**`remote`** is the git remote that `changeset-tag --push` pushes tags to and that the base branch is compared on, `origin` by default."],[42,"**`tagFormat`** names the release tags, `{path}/{version}` by default, like `plugins/alpha/1.2.0`. Empty placeholders leave no leading or repeated slashes in a tag, so a package at the root gets plain version tags, like `1.2.0`, from the same default. Deploy templates, like repository URLs, are filled in as they are. A `gitTagVersion()` version source reads the tags with its own `pattern`, also `{path}/{version}` by default, so set both when you change the format."],[246,"**Commands** in `src/commands/` parse the CLI options and call the library, and `cli.ts` handles the global options, logging and exit codes. Errors carry their exit code, like `ValidationError` with `2`."],[220,"**Credentials**: a GitHub repository URL needs no token in it, since `GITHUB_TOKEN` is sent as an authorization header (see above). A callback can still add credentials for other hosts."],[245,"**Domain logic** is plain functions that take their dependencies as arguments, like `discoverPackages( repository, config )`, so that each one can be tested on its own."],[244,"**External commands** run through injectable runners: `createSvnRunner()` and `createGitRunner()` return functions that spawn the binary without a shell. The classes take a runner as an option and create the default one when it's missing, so tests pass a fake runner, or one that wraps the real runner and records some commands, instead of putting fake binaries on the `PATH`. The `Svn` and `ReleaseRepository` classes wrap a runner with the commands of their domain."],[224,"**Files**: `files.exclude` adds gitignore-style patterns relative to the package directory, and `files.distignore` sets the ignore file relative to it or turns it off with `false`. A `files` at the root of the config applies to every package group, for the rules that all packages share, and the `files` of a group add their patterns after it and can replace its ignore file. Without `files`, each package's `.distignore` applies, like for `zip`; there's no repository-wide `.distignore`, since each one is relative to its package. `zip --package <name>` zips a package with the same files, `getPackageFiles()` returns the directory and the file selection of any configured package, and `getDeployPackages()` lists the packages with deploy targets and their file selection."],[217,"**Git targets** take a `repository` and release both branches and tags by default. `branches` limits the branches, like `[ 'main' ]`, or turns them off with `false`, and `tag` is the tag template in the repository, `{version}` by default, or `false` to not release tags. A release tag is created on top of the default branch of the repository, and a branch is released to the same branch. `history: false` uses the source commit instead of the package history from `git subtree split`, and `message` sets the release commit message, `Release {ref} from {revision}` by default."],[223,"**Packages without a version**: a package entry without `version`, like a single directory with a `glob` of its path, isn't released with changesets, so changes to it don't need one, and it only deploys branches, since it has no release tags."],[36,"**Packages** are the directories that the `glob` of a `packages` entry matches under `root`, with `*`, `?` and `**`, like `plugins/*`, or `.` for a repository with one package at its root. The settings of an entry apply to each of its packages. Discovery never looks inside `.git`, `node_modules`, `vendor` or `dist` directories. A package is identified by its path, or with `idFrom` by the name in its `package.json` or `composer.json`, or with a literal `id` when the group matches exactly one package, and commands that take a package accept a path or identifier."],[238,"**Primitives** work on the directory, file or values they're given and know nothing about the repository: `build <dir>`, `zip <dir>`, `checksums`, `sign`, `verify`, `version-check`, `deploy-git <dir>`, `deploy-svn <dir>`, `i18n-pot <dir>`, `i18n-audit <dir>` and the `i18n-mo`, `i18n-json` and `i18n-php` compilers."],[222,"**Results**: with `--json` or `--github-output`, the result lists each deploy in `deploys` with its `package`, `label`, `target` (the repository or WordPress.org plugin, with credentials redacted), `targetRef` (the tag or branch written there), `version` or `branch`, and its `result` or `error`. A remote target repository also has its `targetHost`, like `github.com`, and `targetPath`, like `me/my-plugin`, without credentials, for a workflow step that calls the API of the forge that hosts it, such as to create the release of the deployed tag. The targets of `--list` have the same `host` and `path`, and `parseRepositoryUrl()` reads them from an HTTP(S), SSH or scp-like URL. `wp-release` doesn't call forge APIs itself."],[219,"**Several targets**: `git` and `svn` each take one target or a list, like a release repository and a mirror on another host. Each target is a separate deploy."],[45,"**Slugs** are a WordPress concept: the folder of a plugin or theme. Only the commands that produce something for WordPress use one: the `{slug}/` root folder and name of a `zip`, the `checksums` manifest, the WordPress.org slug of `deploy-svn`, and `version-check`. Each takes `--slug`, which defaults to the name of the working directory, or of the package directory with `zip --package`, and an SVN deploy target takes `slug`, which defaults to the name of the package directory. Tags and Git deploy targets don't have a slug."],[218,"**SVN targets** only deploy release tags: a stable release syncs `trunk/`, creates its tag and syncs the `assetsDir`, and a pre-release only creates its tag. `slug`, the WordPress.org plugin slug, defaults to the name of the package directory, `url` to the WordPress.org plugin repository, and `message` sets the commit message. Credentials come from `SVN_USERNAME` and `SVN_PASSWORD`, or `--svn-username`."],[221,"**Templates and callbacks**: `repository`, `slug` and `message` are templates with `{name}` and `{path}`, like the `tagFormat`, and `{version}`, `{branch}`, `{ref}` (the tag or branch that the deploy writes in the target) and `{revision}` (the source commit), or callbacks that receive `{ package, version, branch, ref, revision }`, where `package` has the `name`, `directory`, `path` and `type`. A callback can name a target after the package directory, choose another host for some packages, read the environment, or return nothing to skip the target for that package or ref; an empty `message` uses the default one. Credentials in URLs are never printed or listed."],[37,"**Templates** name the tags and the deploy targets of a package with these placeholders:"],[239,"**The config layer** reads `wp-release.config.js` for everything about the repository: where packages are (`root` and the package globs), how their versions and tags are named (`version`, `tagFormat`), which files they ship (`files`), how their translations are extracted (`i18n`), where they deploy (`deploy`) and the git `remote`. The changeset commands (with a single-project fallback when no config exists), `deploy-git` and `deploy-svn --release-ref`, `build --package` and `zip --package`, and `i18n-pot` and `i18n-audit` with `--package` or `--all` belong to it, and the global `--config` and `--tag-format` options select or adjust the config. `zip` and `checksums` also read caller configuration for integrity settings even without `--package`; verification reads it only when explicitly selected. Only `--release-ref` reads which package and version a ref releases. A primitive with a config mode, like `deploy-git --release-ref` beside `deploy-git <dir>` or `zip --package` beside `zip <dir>`, only takes the package directory, its files and its targets from the config there, and otherwise works the same."],[243,"**Workflows with steps** are classes, so that their steps share state and dependencies on the instance: `SvnDeploy` shares the temporary working copy across `prepare()`, `checkout()`, `sync()` and `commit()`, `GitDeploy` binds the options and the git runner to its steps, and `ChangesetRelease` binds the repository and the runners to `add()`, `version()` and `tag()`."],[380,"`--checksums-algorithms md5,sha256` selects JSON digest fields; lists replace configured lists. Supported algorithms are MD5 (for compatibility) and SHA-256. The `sha256sum` codec requires SHA-256. The standalone `checksums` command also accepts `--checksums-format`, keeping `--output` relative to the working directory."],[376,"`--checksums-file <file>` enables checksums and sets a relative filename independently of the ZIP name. Without `--embed-checksums`, it resolves against the ZIP output directory; with embedding, it resolves against the package root inside the ZIP (the archive root with `--no-root-folder`). Nested paths are supported, but absolute paths, backslashes, drive letters, control characters and dot segments are rejected. Embedding emits no external manifest and never modifies the source directory. Embedded minisign signatures contain a signing timestamp, so ZIP bytes can differ across signing times; unsigned metadata and WordPress/SSH signatures remain deterministic. Selected source files must not collide with generated manifest or signature entries."],[379,"`--checksums-format` chooses the manifest format. `wordpress-json` (default) uses the WordPress-compatible structure shown above. `sha256sum` uses GNU-compatible SHA-256 lines and escaping, with `SHA256SUMS` as its default filename. It carries no package identity or version, and standard `sha256sum --check` checks listed files without rejecting additional files; `wp-release verify` also checks for extra files."],[360,"`--checksums` also writes the checksums manifest of the archived files next to the ZIP, with `.checksums.json` instead of `.zip`."],[353,"`--checksums` writes `<output-dir>.checksums.json` beside the directory and hashes the copied build files. `--sign-checksums` generates and signs that manifest, implying `--checksums`. Use `--slug` and `--version` to override its metadata. The manifest and its signatures are excluded from the source selection, so rebuilding with `--force` does not package previous sidecars. Existing manifests and requested signature files also require `--force`; signing keys, formats and signature destinations are validated before creating or replacing the build. `--sign` is unsupported because a directory has no single file to sign."],[417,"`--constant NAME` (repeatable) checks `define( 'NAME', '1.2.0' )` or `const NAME = '1.2.0'` in every PHP file outside `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot directories. A constant that isn't defined is a mismatch."],[210,"`--dry-run` runs everything except the push. `--message` sets the commit message, which defaults to `Release <tag or branch> from <source commit>`. Credentials come from git, like a credential helper or an SSH key, and credentials in URLs are never printed. For an HTTPS repository on `github.com`, or on the host of `GITHUB_SERVER_URL` like GitHub Enterprise Server, the `GITHUB_TOKEN` environment variable is sent as an authorization header, like `actions/checkout` does, so the token stays out of the URL, the git arguments and the repository config, and it replaces the header that `actions/checkout` persists. Pass `token` to `resolveGitDeployOptions()` for another HTTPS host. The commit needs a git identity (`user.name` and `user.email`)."],[358,"`--filename-format` changes the name, like `--filename-format {slug}` for `my-plugin.zip`."],[191,"`--from-zip` reads the files from a ZIP instead of `<source-dir>`. The ZIP is checked before it's extracted: entries with absolute paths, `..` segments or symbolic links, and entries outside a single `{slug}/` root folder, are rejected."],[418,"`--git-tag` defaults to `GITHUB_REF` when it's a tag. `--git-tag-format` (default `{version}`) can have a prefix or the slug, like `v{version}` or `{slug}-{version}`; a leading `v` in the version is ignored, like for SVN tags."],[364,"`--package <name>` zips a package of the configured packages, by identifier or path under the configured root like `plugins/alpha`, instead of a source directory: its directory with the `files` config of its package group, the same files that its deploys select. The slug defaults to the directory name and the version to the `Version` header, and `--slug` and `--version` still set them. Any package of the config works, with or without a version or deploy targets. To zip what a deploy just released, pass the `package` and `version` of the `deploy-git --release-ref` result, in the same job and from the same build, so the ZIP has exactly the files of the release tag in the release repository. It can't be combined with `<source-dir>` or the file selection options. The `--json` result has the `package`, `slug` and `version`. `zipPackage()` builds the same ZIP from a script, with `getPackageFiles()` for the directory and the files of a package."],[303,"`--package` selects one configured package and permits extraction-specific overrides; `--all` requires configuration and selects enabled WordPress packages only. Source directories, package selectors and `--all` are mutually exclusive. Config-free directory mode reads only its explicit options and metadata; it does not import repository config implicitly. A disabled package fails when explicitly selected. Configuration uses `i18n` at the root and on package entries, including an exact `glob` for an individual package, following the existing group model. Package entries remain non-overlapping; there is no additional package manifest configuration source."],[194,"`--readme-only` can't be combined with `--svn-tag`, since a tag is a release snapshot that a readme update never changes."],[357,"`--root-folder <name>` changes the root folder and `--no-root-folder` puts the files at the root of the ZIP."],[377,"`--sign-checksums` enables checksums and signs only the manifest, before embedding when requested. It reads `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY` and uses `--sign-format wordpress,minisign,ssh` to select formats; each signature travels with the manifest. `--sign-format` selects formats for explicitly requested signing targets and never enables signing. `--sign` signs only the final ZIP; `--sign-checksums` controls the manifest in either placement mode. Use both flags to sign both targets. `--force` permits replacing external output files and signatures. `--json` reports external manifests in `checksumsPath`, embedded package-relative paths in `checksumsEmbedded`, and manifest signatures in `checksumsSignatures`."],[362,"`--sign-format` selects formats for requested signatures, such as `--sign-format wordpress,minisign,ssh`. It requires `--sign` or `--sign-checksums` and never enables signing by itself. Signing keys, formats and existing signature destinations are validated before writing the ZIP or manifest. `--force` also overwrites requested signatures. Job summaries include signatures and public keys; JSON results include `signatures` and `publicKey`."],[361,"`--sign` signs only the ZIP with `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY`. `--sign-checksums` generates and signs only the checksums manifest, implying `--checksums`. Use both flags to sign both files. External signatures are written alongside their files; embedded manifest signatures are written inside the package. The default format is WordPress `.sig`, unless integrity configuration selects another format."],[197,"`--trunk` without `--svn-tag` works but warns, to remind you that updating trunk alone doesn't create a version tag for stable downloads."],[403,"`--verbose` prints the public key of the signing key in the form of each written format. `--json` returns it as `publicKey`, and in every form as `publicKeys`. Pass it to `verify --public-key` and to the plugin that checks the signatures."],[195,"`--version` defaults to `--svn-tag`, then the `Version` header of the deployed files; when `--version` and `--svn-tag` are both set they must match."],[171,"`--version` defaults to the `Version` header in `zip`, `checksums` and `version-check`, and `checksums --type` defaults to the detected package type."],[394,"`--zip <file|url>` checks the files of a ZIP instead of a directory. The ZIP can be a URL, such as the one that WordPress.org builds for a release, and its single root folder is found automatically (or set `--root-folder`). The ZIP is extracted to a temporary directory after the same safety checks as `deploy-svn --from-zip`."],[169,"`.distignore` and `--exclude` use full gitignore syntax, including `!` negation. `--exclude-gitattributes` asks Git to evaluate `export-ignore` attributes, including nested `.gitattributes` files, then adds those paths to the exclusions. It is off by default, so releases don't unexpectedly depend on Git or change selection when a repository adds attributes. Git must be installed and the source directory must be inside a working tree; the flag is ignored by `deploy-svn --from-zip`, since the ZIP is already filtered. Version control metadata (`.git`, `.svn` and `.hg`, at any depth, including the `.git` files of worktrees and submodules) is always left out, also without an ignore file and when deploying from a ZIP, and no `!` pattern can re-include it. This keeps a repository's history out of ZIPs and deploys when the source directory is the repository root. The comma-separated `--exclude` list works like `--exclude` in `wp i18n make-pot`. Selected symbolic links are an error, since ZIP archives and WordPress.org SVN don't support them."],[133,"`@wpelevator/wp-release` is the suite of release tools and `wp-release` is its CLI. \"Release\" is the whole workflow; each command is one operation in it. Commands are flat: a single word or dashed words like `version-check` and `deploy-svn`, without command groups or aliases. Each GitHub Action is named `wp-release-` followed by the command name, so the CLI command and the `uses:` name always match:"],[283,"`@wpelevator/wp-release` provides a CLI and library for building release directories and ZIPs, creating checksums and signatures, checking versions, extracting and compiling translations, and deploying to WordPress.org SVN or Git repositories."],[351,"`build` and `zip` use the same internal helper to load file rules, exclude their outputs, select release files and reject an empty selection. `build` copies the selected files directly into `--output <dir>`, without a slug wrapper or ZIP. Checksums and manifest signatures are optional. It supports the shared file selection flags above and `--package <name>` with `--config <path>` for configured package rules. A configured package cannot be combined with a source directory or file selection flags."],[231,"`build` compiles `src/` to `dist/` with `tsc`, leaving out the tests."],[164,"`build`, `zip`, `checksums`, `deploy-svn` and `deploy-git` select files the same way:"],[67,"`changeset-version --prerelease <id>` (the `prerelease` option of `versionChangesets()`) releases pre-releases of the next versions, like `1.3.0-rc.1` for a minor bump of `1.2.0`. The identifier, like `alpha`, `beta` or `rc`, can have a number, like `rc.2`, which is used as is and fails when the version is already tagged. Without a number, the number is one above the highest existing tag of that identifier and next stable version, read with the `tagFormat` of the package, so the first one is `rc.1`, and the numbering starts again at `.1` when later changesets raise the next version. The dependents of a source become pre-releases too, so that they bundle its pre-release, unless they're left out with `--no-dependents`."],[71,"`changeset-version --snapshot <id>` (the `snapshot` option of `versionChangesets()`) releases snapshots of the next versions for test builds, like `1.3.0-pr-251.20261012183005` for a pull request: the next version with the identifier, like `pr-251` or `pr-251.0abc123` with a commit hash, and the UTC time, so that every snapshot is unique and they sort by time. Snapshots aren't numbered from tags and can't be combined with `--prerelease`. Like a pre-release, a snapshot writes every version target except the readme `Stable tag`. Build and publish the versioned files without committing them, for example under an npm dist-tag like `pr-251`, since a snapshot consumes the changesets:"],[327,"`ChangesetRelease` runs the `changeset-*` commands: `add()`, `getStatus()`, `version()` and `tag()`, plus `getPlan()`, `getPackages()` and `getConfiguredPackages()`. Each call reads the configuration, packages and pending changesets again, so one instance can version and then tag. `getConfiguredPackages()` returns package directories with their resolved release group metadata; `formatTag()` and `formatTemplate()` apply the same placeholders as the release workflows. It takes a git runner from `createGitRunner()` as `git`, so that tests can replace it. Without a config file, it detects a single root project. Its optional `singleProject` settings accept `versions` (the existing version descriptors), `versionFrom` (the same selector as `--version-from`), `baseBranch`, `remote`, `changesets`, `readmeChangelog` and `changelogInsertAfter`; `tagFormat` remains a repository option. The functions `addChangeset()`, `getChangesetStatus()`, `versionChangesets()`, `tagChangesets()`, `getChangesetReleasePlan()`, `getChangesetPackages()` and `getConfiguredPackages()` do the same with the default runners, in one call."],[373,"`checksums` writes a manifest with the MD5 and SHA-256 hash of every selected file, in the format of the WordPress.org plugin checksums API (`https://downloads.wordpress.org/plugin-checksums/{slug}/{version}.json`). Paths are relative to the plugin or theme folder, sorted, and use forward slashes:"],[58,"`dependents.sections` lists the npm dependency sections that declare a source, defaulting to `dependencies` and `optionalDependencies`; add `devDependencies` for dependencies that end up in the dependent's build, or `peerDependencies` to scan them."],[211,"`deploy-git --release-ref <ref>` and `deploy-svn --release-ref <ref>` deploy the packages of a monorepo, or of a single-package repository, to the targets in the `deploy` config of their package groups, like the release repository of each package or its WordPress.org plugin. The ref decides what's deployed: a release tag of a package, like `refs/tags/plugins/alpha/1.2.0` read with the `tagFormat`, deploys that package and version, and a branch, like `refs/heads/main`, deploys every package with a target for that branch. A name without `refs/` is a release tag when it matches the tag format of a package, otherwise a branch, and in GitHub Actions `$GITHUB_REF` is the pushed ref."],[199,"`deploy-git` deploys the selected files of a plugin, theme or package to a Git repository, like a distribution repository that ships the built files and `vendor/` for Composer installs or GitHub-hosted updates, or the release repository of a monorepo package. It selects files like `zip`, with `.distignore` and `--exclude`, so built files that git ignores in the source repository, like `build/` or `vendor/`, are released too. The source directory must be inside a Git repository, which provides the source history and stores the release objects before they're pushed."],[216,"`deploy-svn` has the same modes. `--release-ref` can't be combined with the options of a single target, like `--git-url`, `--tag` or `--svn-tag`, which come from the config."],[177,"`deploy-svn` syncs a plugin to the WordPress.org plugin SVN repository. Each SVN write is a separate flag and nothing is written unless requested. All writes go into one commit."],[236,"`execute( 'deploy-svn', args, { cwd, logger, svnRunner } )` runs a single-plugin deployment or a configured plan or deployment. The optional `svnRunner` replaces the external command runner in both modes; without it, the command uses its normal runner."],[330,"`GitHubActionsWriter` prints workflow commands, so that notices, warnings and errors with a file are annotations, and appends summaries to `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`. It's the CLI's `github` log format, and the GitHub Actions pass it to their logger. Its `workspace` and `summaryPath` options default to `GITHUB_WORKSPACE` and `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`."],[301,"`i18n-pot` extracts plugin/theme strings and writes a reproducible UTF-8 POT. `i18n-audit` reads the same source and returns findings without writing files. Both run with Node alone: no PHP, WordPress installation, database, WP-CLI or network requests. Extraction is independent of `files`, `.distignore` and Git `export-ignore` distribution rules."],[233,"`lint` runs ESLint and a `tsc` type check."],[335,"`loadFileRules()` reads the ignore file and `--exclude` patterns into a gitignore matcher, optionally adds Git `export-ignore` paths, and `selectFiles()` lists the files it doesn't ignore. `compareChecksums()` compares two hash maps without reading any files, and `parseChecksums()` validates manifest JSON. `extractZip()` extracts a ZIP after the same checks as `deploy-svn --from-zip`."],[328,"`Logger` filters messages by level and passes them to a writer, like the transports of other Node.js loggers. The writer decides how messages look:"],[410,"`minisign` signs the BLAKE2b-512 digest of the file, like `minisign -S` does by default, and adds a trusted comment with the signing time and file name that a second signature covers. minisign generates a random key ID for its keys, but the key here is a plain Ed25519 seed, so wp-release derives the key ID from the public key. `wp-release verify` doesn't compare key IDs, so signatures of keys that minisign generated verify too."],[323,"`resolveDeployOptions()` applies the defaults and throws a `ValidationError` (with `exitCode` `2`) for invalid combinations. `SvnDeploy` exposes each step (`prepare()`, `checkVersions()`, `checkout()`, `sync()`, `commit()` and `cleanup()`) separately so CI wrappers can run and report them one by one; `run()` runs all of them, or nothing when there's nothing to write. `getPlan()` lists the planned SVN changes. The `Svn` class wraps the svn command line, and takes the command runner as a dependency so that tests can replace it."],[325,"`resolveGitDeployOptions()` throws a `ValidationError` without a repository URL or with an invalid branch or tag. `GitDeploy` exposes `createTree()`, `fetchPrevious()`, `createRelease()`, `isOutdated()`, `checkTag()`, `getRefsToPush()` and `push()` separately, like `SvnDeploy`, and takes a git command runner from `createGitRunner()` as a dependency. The result has `outdated` when the branch wasn't moved for older source history, the refs to write in `planned` and the refs that were pushed in `pushed`, which is empty in a dry run. `redactUrls()` hides the credentials in URLs."],[400,"`sign` writes a detached signature next to each file, `<file>.sig`, so that downloads and installed copies can be trusted. Sign both the ZIP and the checksums manifest: the ZIP signature covers installs and updates, and the manifest signature covers the contents after unpacking and copies that weren't built by you."],[411,"`ssh` is the armored `SSHSIG` format that `ssh-keygen -Y sign` writes, with a SHA-512 digest and the `file` namespace, so that the signature can't pass as one for commits or another namespace. It's written to `.sshsig` instead of the `.sig` of `ssh-keygen`, which the `wordpress` format uses. Put the SSH public key in an `allowed_signers` file as `<identity> ssh-ed25519 AAAA…`."],[232,"`test` runs the `.ts` tests directly with `node --test`, using Node.js type stripping, so it needs Node.js 22.18 or later but no build. The SVN integration tests in `svn-deploy.integration.test.ts` create repositories with `svnadmin` and deploy to their `file://` URLs; they're skipped when `svn` or `svnadmin` isn't installed."],[329,"`TextWriter` prints plain text to `stdout` and `stderr`. It's the default."],[104,"`verify` annotates each changed file and each file that isn't in the checksums."],[390,"`verify` checks a directory against a manifest from `checksums` or from the WordPress.org checksums API, and reports every changed, missing and extra file. It exits with `2` unless all files match:"],[392,"`verify` doesn't read `.distignore`, so that an ignore file inside the checked directory can't hide added files. `--exclude <patterns>` skips paths in both the directory and the manifest, for files that aren't part of the release, such as caches."],[103,"`version-check` and `deploy-svn` annotate each version mismatch on the file and line of the version: the `Version` header, the readme `Stable tag` or the PHP constant."],[414,"`version-check` compares the versions that have to agree before a release, and reports every mismatch with exit code `2`:"],[32,"`version.from` selects the current version; `version.to` lists release targets. Use `phpConstantVersion( { file, constant } )` to sync a PHP constant."],[405,"`zip --sign` signs only the ZIP; add `--sign-checksums` to sign its manifest too. `signFiles()` signs files from a script."],[383,"`zip` and `checksums` read the caller's release configuration. For verification of custom codecs or configured filenames, pass `--config wp-release.config.mjs` explicitly. See Verify."],[386,"`zip` and `deploy-svn` carry the pair like any other file. Don't use `zip --checksums` here, since it would write a second manifest that lists the first one. Attach `build/checksums.json` and `build/checksums.json.sig` to the GitHub release as they are."],[356,"`zip` writes `{slug}.{version}.zip` to the current directory, or to `--output <file>`, with the selected files in a `{slug}/` root folder:"],[138,"| Capability                      | CLI command         | GitHub Action |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------- | ------------- |\n| Verify files against a manifest | `wp-release verify` |               |"],[139,"| Capability                    | CLI command                | GitHub Action |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------- |\n| Check that the versions agree | `wp-release version-check` |               |"],[140,"| Capability                  | CLI command             | GitHub Action                      |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| Deploy to WordPress.org SVN | `wp-release deploy-svn` | `wpelevator/wp-release-deploy-svn` |"],[136,"| Capability                  | CLI command            | GitHub Action                     |\n| --------------------------- | ---------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Create a checksums manifest | `wp-release checksums` | `wpelevator/wp-release-checksums` |"],[141,"| Capability                 | CLI command             | GitHub Action |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------- |\n| Deploy to a Git repository | `wp-release deploy-git` |               |"],[134,"| Capability        | CLI command        | GitHub Action |\n| ----------------- | ------------------ | ------------- |\n| Build a directory | `wp-release build` |               |"],[135,"| Capability  | CLI command      | GitHub Action               |\n| ----------- | ---------------- | --------------------------- |\n| Build a ZIP | `wp-release zip` | `wpelevator/wp-release-zip` |"],[137,"| Capability | CLI command       | GitHub Action                |\n| ---------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Sign files | `wp-release sign` | `wpelevator/wp-release-sign` |"],[321,"| Code | Meaning                                                                                                                                          |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `2`  | Invalid options, failed release checks, an existing SVN tag, a Git tag that exists with another release, or files that don't match the checksums |"],[322,"| Code | Meaning                                                   |\n| ---- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `3`  | Missing SVN credentials, or credentials that SVN rejected |"],[320,"| Code | Meaning                                                 |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `1`  | Unexpected failure, such as a failed svn or git command |"],[319,"| Code | Meaning                               |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `0`  | Success, dry run, no changes, or help |"],[84,"| Command                                                                 | Description                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-git --release-ref <ref> [--package <name>] [--list]` | Deploy the configured packages of a release tag or branch to their Git targets. |"],[85,"| Command                                                                 | Description                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-svn --release-ref <ref> [--package <name>] [--list]` | Deploy the configured plugin of a release tag to WordPress.org. |"],[83,"| Command                                                          | Description                                                                             |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-git [<source-dir>] --git-url <url> [options]` | Deploy the files of a plugin, theme or package to a Git repository as a release commit. |"],[80,"| Command                                                         | Description                                                                |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release verify [<source-dir>] --checksums <file> [options]` | Check a plugin or theme directory, or a ZIP, against a checksums manifest. |"],[89,"| Command                                                      | Description                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-json <file.po> --domain <domain> [options]` | Compile the JavaScript strings of a translated PO file into WordPress script JSON files. |"],[75,"| Command                                                    | Description                                   |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release build [<source-dir>] --output <dir> [options]` | Copy selected release files into a directory. |"],[81,"| Command                                             | Description                                                                            |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release version-check [<source-dir>] [options]` | Check that the `Version` header, readme `Stable tag`, PHP constants and git tag agree. |"],[82,"| Command                                          | Description                                                 |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release deploy-svn [<source-dir>] [options]` | Deploy a plugin to the WordPress.org plugin SVN repository. |"],[87,"| Command                                          | Description                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-audit [<source-dir>] [options]` | Report translation string problems without writing files. |"],[77,"| Command                                         | Description                                                                         |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release checksums [<source-dir>] [options]` | Write the MD5 and SHA-256 hashes of every distributed file to a checksums manifest. |"],[86,"| Command                                        | Description                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-pot [<source-dir>] [options]` | Extract translatable strings into a reproducible POT file. |"],[76,"| Command                                   | Description                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release zip [<source-dir>] [options]` | Create a ZIP archive with a `{slug}/` root folder, respecting `.distignore`. |"],[90,"| Command                                   | Description                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-php <file.po> [options]` | Compile a translated PO file into a WordPress `.l10n.php` file. |"],[93,"| Command                                  | Description                                |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp-release changeset-version [options]` | Apply version bumps and changelog entries. |"],[88,"| Command                                  | Description                           |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release i18n-mo <file.po> [options]` | Compile a translated PO file into MO. |"],[92,"| Command                                 | Description                                                      |\n| --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release changeset-status [options]` | List pending releases and check changed packages for changesets. |"],[78,"| Command                               | Description                                                                                                            |\n| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release sign <file>... [options]` | Sign files with Ed25519, writing a detached `<file>.sig` next to each, or minisign and SSH signatures with `--format`. |"],[91,"| Command                              | Description                                  |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-release changeset-add [options]` | Record the release impact of a pull request. |"],[94,"| Command                              | Description                          |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |\n| `wp-release changeset-tag [options]` | Create missing package release tags. |"],[79,"| Command             | Description                                                                                      |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp-release keygen` | Generate a base64 Ed25519 private seed and its public key, also as minisign and SSH public keys. |"],[115,"| Command             | Summary                                                               |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-version` | A table of the package releases with their version changes and bumps. |"],[114,"| Command            | Summary                                               |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-status` | Each changed package with whether it has a changeset. |"],[116,"| Command         | Summary                                                                                  |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-tag` | Each tag with its previous tag and status, and its release notes in a collapsed section. |"],[113,"| Command         | Summary                                                                        |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `version-check` | The matching version, or a table of the mismatches with their files and lines. |"],[106,"| Command      | Summary                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deploy-svn` | Plugin, linked to its WordPress.org page, version, written SVN paths, the commit linked to its WordPress.org changeset (or the dry run or no-op result), and the SVN changes in a collapsed table. |"],[107,"| Command      | Summary                                                   |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deploy-git` | Repository, release commit and the pushed branch and tag. |"],[110,"| Command     | Summary                       |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `checksums` | Manifest and number of files. |"],[112,"| Command  | Summary                                                                                  |\n| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `verify` | That all files match, or the counts and a table of the changed, missing and extra files. |"],[109,"| Command | Summary                                                                                                                |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `zip`   | ZIP, number of files and checksums manifest; with `--sign`, signature files and the public key of each written format. |"],[108,"| Command | Summary                                                                                               |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `build` | Output directory, number of copied files and optional manifest; requested signatures and public keys. |"],[111,"| Command | Summary                                                    |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sign`  | Signature files and the public key of each written format. |"],[147,"| Concept                         | CLI                           | GitHub Action                  | Library         | Meaning                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------ | --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Checksums filename when zipping | `zip --checksums-file <file>` | `checksums-file` in ZIP action | `checksumsFile` | Relative manifest filename beside the ZIP or inside its package root when embedded. |"],[153,"| Concept                      | CLI                               | GitHub Action | Library         | Meaning                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Branch and tag to release to | `--branch <name>`, `--tag <name>` |               | `branch`, `tag` | Refs written by `deploy-git` in the Git repository, distinct from the git tag that `version-check` compares. |"],[143,"| Concept                   | CLI                                       | GitHub Action | Library     | Meaning                                                                           |\n| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Plugin or theme directory | `<source-dir>` argument or `--source-dir` | `source-dir`  | `sourceDir` | Directory that a command reads, whether it's the repository or a build directory. |"],[146,"| Concept                   | CLI                         | GitHub Action                | Library                              | Meaning                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------- | --------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Standalone checksums file | `checksums --output <file>` | `output` in checksums action | first argument of `writeChecksums()` | Manifest destination relative to the working directory; the CLI returns its absolute path as `checksumsPath`. |"],[159,"| Concept               | CLI                   | GitHub Action               | Library | Meaning                                                             |\n| --------------------- | --------------------- | --------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Release ref to deploy | `--release-ref <ref>` | `release-ref` in SVN action | `ref`   | Read configured packages and deployment targets from a release ref. |"],[158,"| Concept            | CLI                     | GitHub Action              | Library     | Meaning                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------- | -------------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Release tag format | `--tag-format <format>` | `tag-format` in SVN action | `tagFormat` | How `release-ref` identifies a package and its version. |"],[149,"| Concept            | CLI               | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning                             |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| Git tag to compare | `--git-tag <tag>` |               | `gitTag` | Git tag checked by `version-check`. |"],[150,"| Concept           | CLI                   | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning                                               |\n| ----------------- | --------------------- | ------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| SVN tag to create | `--svn-tag <version>` | `svn-tag`     | `svnTag` | Tag written by `deploy-svn`, distinct from a git tag. |"],[155,"| Concept         | CLI                                                                 | GitHub Action                  | Library                      | Meaning                                                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| SVN credentials | `--svn-username <name>`, `SVN_USERNAME` and `SVN_PASSWORD` env vars | `svn-username`, `svn-password` | `svnUsername`, `svnPassword` | Needed only to commit. There's no password flag, since it would show in the process list; the action inputs and library options fall back to the env vars. |"],[144,"| Concept         | CLI                    | GitHub Action | Library     | Meaning                                                    |\n| --------------- | ---------------------- | ------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Build directory | `build --output <dir>` | —             | `outputDir` | Directory written by `build`; files go directly inside it. |"],[154,"| Concept        | CLI                    | GitHub Action | Library       | Meaning                                                                                                                       |\n| -------------- | ---------------------- | ------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Source history | `--split-prefix <dir>` |               | `splitPrefix` | Directory of a monorepo package whose history, from `git subtree split`, becomes the source history of `deploy-git` releases. |"],[157,"| Concept        | CLI               | GitHub Action                              | Library      | Meaning                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Release config | `--config <path>` | `config` in ZIP, checksums and SVN actions | `configFile` | Explicit JavaScript config path for package selection, integrity settings or deployment. |"],[152,"| Concept        | CLI               | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning                                                                                                               |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Git repository | `--git-url <url>` |               | `gitUrl` | Destination of `deploy-git`. Credentials come from git, like a token in the URL or an SSH key, and are never printed. |"],[151,"| Concept        | CLI               | GitHub Action | Library  | Meaning             |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------- | -------- | ------------------- |\n| SVN repository | `--svn-url <url>` | `svn-url`     | `svnUrl` | Deploy destination. |"],[148,"| Concept       | CLI                 | GitHub Action | Library   | Meaning                                 |\n| ------------- | ------------------- | ------------- | --------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| ZIP to deploy | `--from-zip <file>` | `from-zip`    | `fromZip` | Existing ZIP read as the deploy source. |"],[160,"| Concept     | CLI                   | GitHub Action           | Library  | Meaning                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------- | --------------------- | ----------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Assets sync | `--no-assets` to skip | `assets: false` to skip | `assets` | Booleans are positive in the library and the action; `--no-assets` is only the CLI negation, never `noAssets` or a `no-assets` input. |"],[156,"| Concept    | CLI                                                | GitHub Action   | Library                                                   | Meaning                                                                                                                               |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Log format | `--log-format <text\\|github>`, `--ci` for `github` | always `github` | `writer: new TextWriter()` or `new GitHubActionsWriter()` | How the log is printed: plain text, or GitHub Actions annotations and job summaries. Each name matches the start of its writer class. |"],[145,"| Concept  | CLI                   | GitHub Action          | Library   | Meaning                                                           |\n| -------- | --------------------- | ---------------------- | --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| ZIP file | `zip --output <file>` | `output` in ZIP action | `zipPath` | Destination passed to `zipPackage()`; also returned as `zipPath`. |"],[19,"| Flag                                       | Version descriptor                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-php-constant <file>#<constant>` | PHP `define()` or `const` version source and target |"],[18,"| Flag                                    | Version descriptor                                                                                        |\n| --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-wordpress-stable-tag <file>` | WordPress readme Stable tag source and target; keeps its stable version during pre-releases and snapshots |"],[16,"| Flag                                | Version descriptor                                |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-wordpress-plugin <file>` | WordPress plugin Version header source and target |"],[17,"| Flag                               | Version descriptor                               |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `--version-wordpress-theme <file>` | WordPress theme Version header source and target |"],[15,"| Flag                             | Version descriptor                                                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `--version-composer-json <file>` | Composer manifest version source and target; a target without a `version` field is skipped, while a source must have one |"],[14,"| Flag                            | Version descriptor                     |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-package-json <file>` | npm manifest version source and target |"],[20,"| Flag                          | Version descriptor                                                                                                      |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--version-git-tag <pattern>` | Current version from existing stable Git tags, with a pattern such as `v{version}`; source-only, never a version target |"],[180,"| Flag                  | Default | Writes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| --------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--svn-tag <version>` | off     | Create `tags/<version>`. With `--trunk`, the tag is a copy of the synced `trunk/`. Without it, the tag is built directly from the source files and `trunk/` is left alone, for pre-release tags. A leading `v` is stripped, so `v1.2.0` creates `tags/1.2.0`. |"],[181,"| Flag                 | Default | Writes                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| -------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--assets-dir <dir>` | not set | Sync this directory with banners, icons and screenshots to the SVN `assets/` directory. There's no default directory, so assets are only deployed when this is set, and it must exist. |"],[369,"| Flag               | `zip`                           | `build`                         |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `--sign-checksums` | Generate and sign the manifest. | Generate and sign the manifest. |"],[200,"| Flag              | Default | Writes                          |\n| ----------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `--branch <name>` | off     | Push the release to this branch |"],[370,"| Flag            | `zip`                                               | `build`                            |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `--sign-format` | Choose formats for explicitly requested signatures. | Same; requires `--sign-checksums`. |"],[179,"| Flag            | Default | Writes                                                                                                                             |\n| --------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--readme-only` | off     | Sync only the readme to `trunk/`, for updates between releases such as bumping `Tested up to`. Can't be combined with `--svn-tag`. |"],[201,"| Flag           | Default | Writes                          |\n| -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `--tag <name>` | off     | Create this tag for the release |"],[367,"| Flag          | `zip`                                         | `build`                                             |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--checksums` | Generate an unsigned manifest beside the ZIP. | Generate an unsigned manifest beside the directory. |"],[182,"| Flag          | Default | Writes                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--no-assets` |         | Skip the `--assets-dir` sync for one run, for example when a config always passes `--assets-dir`. |"],[178,"| Flag      | Default | Writes                                     |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `--trunk` | off     | Sync the full source directory to `trunk/` |"],[368,"| Flag     | `zip`              | `build`      |\n| -------- | ------------------ | ------------ |\n| `--sign` | Sign only the ZIP. | Unsupported. |"],[407,"| Format      | File         | Checked by                                                                        |\n| ----------- | ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wordpress` | `<file>.sig` | WordPress core's `verify_file_signature()`, update-client and `wp-release verify` |"],[408,"| Format     | File             | Checked by                                                                    |\n| ---------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `minisign` | `<file>.minisig` | minisign and compatible tools: `minisign -Vm <file> -P <minisign public key>` |"],[409,"| Format | File            | Checked by                                                                                                      |\n| ------ | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ssh`  | `<file>.sshsig` | OpenSSH 8.1 or later: `ssh-keygen -Y verify -f allowed_signers -I <identity> -n file -s <file>.sshsig < <file>` |"],[100,"| Log format | Output                                                                                                              |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `github`   | GitHub Actions annotations and job summaries. The default when `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true`. `--ci` is a shorthand for it. |"],[99,"| Log format | Output                                          |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `text`     | Plain text. The default outside GitHub Actions. |"],[215,"| Mode             | Command                                      | Use                                                        |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Single directory | `deploy-git <dir> --git-url <url> [options]` | Deploy one directory without a config, as described above. |"],[213,"| Mode        | Command                                           | Use                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| One package | `deploy-git --release-ref <ref> --package <name>` | Deploy only that package (repeatable), by identifier or path, for example to retry a failed one or in a CI job for each package with its own credentials. |"],[214,"| Mode | Command                                 | Use                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ---- | --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Plan | `deploy-git --release-ref <ref> --list` | List the deploys without running them, with each package, its version or branch and the targets with the tag or branch they get. With `--json` or `--github-output` the result is `{ packages: [ … ] }`, for a CI matrix with `include: ${{ fromJSON( steps.plan.outputs.result ).packages }}`. |"],[212,"| Mode | Command                          | Use                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ---- | -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| All  | `deploy-git --release-ref <ref>` | Deploy every package of the ref to each of its targets, one at a time, in one log group for each package. A failed deploy doesn't stop the others, and the command exits with `1` at the end. |"],[276,"| Module                                                  | Responsibility                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `po.ts`, `translation-json.ts` and `translation-php.ts` | Parsing translated PO files and compiling MO, script JSON and `.l10n.php`. |"],[271,"| Module                                      | Responsibility                                                                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `php.ts`, `javascript.ts` and `metadata.ts` | Parsers for PHP, JavaScript and TypeScript, and `block.json`, `theme.json` and plugin and theme headers. |"],[274,"| Module                           | Responsibility                                               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `audit.ts` and `placeholders.ts` | Audit findings and the sprintf placeholders that they check. |"],[254,"| Module                    | Responsibility                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-dependents.ts` | The dependents policy: which packages re-release with their sources, and rewriting their dependency specs. |"],[252,"| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                                             |\n| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-packages.ts` | Discovering the packages of the configured globs, with or without a version, and finding the changed ones. |"],[262,"| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `release-repository.ts` | `ReleaseRepository`, the git commands of a release: tags, commits and changed files. |"],[249,"| Module                 | Responsibility                                                                                                                              |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-release.ts` | `ChangesetRelease`, which plans releases and runs `add()`, `getStatus()`, `version()` and `tag()`, and the function form of each operation. |"],[251,"| Module                | Responsibility                                                                            |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-config.ts` | Loading and validating `wp-release.config.js`, and the version source and target helpers. |"],[253,"| Module               | Responsibility                                  |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `changeset-files.ts` | Reading, writing and consuming changeset files. |"],[260,"| Module              | Responsibility                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `package-deploy.ts` | Resolving the configured deploys of a ref: the released packages, their targets and file selections, for `deploy-git` and `deploy-svn`, and the file selection of a package for `zip --package`. |"],[250,"| Module              | Responsibility                                                                                       |\n| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `single-project.ts` | Resolving the config-free root project, its version source and targets, and shared release settings. |"],[256,"| Module             | Responsibility                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `version-files.ts` | Reading and writing versions in manifests, headers, readmes and PHP constants. |"],[277,"| Module             | Responsibility                                                 |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `atomic-output.ts` | Validating output paths and replacing output files atomically. |"],[258,"| Module            | Responsibility                                                                      |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `release-tags.ts` | Choosing the version and previous tag of a release, and building its release notes. |"],[263,"| Module            | Responsibility                                                                     |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `file-journal.ts` | `FileJournal`, which restores every file a failed `changeset-version` run changed. |"],[261,"| Module           | Responsibility                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `package-zip.ts` | `zipPackage()`, the ZIP and checksums manifest of the selected files of a package, for `zip` and the snapshot builds of a monorepo. |"],[272,"| Module          | Responsibility                                                        |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `extraction.ts` | The translation functions and their arguments, shared by the parsers. |"],[255,"| Module          | Responsibility                                            |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `changesets.ts` | Parsing and formatting changesets, and the release types. |"],[259,"| Module          | Responsibility                            |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `tag-format.ts` | Formatting tag names from the tag format. |"],[257,"| Module         | Responsibility                                                           |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `changelog.ts` | Changelog entries, and the `CHANGELOG.md` and readme changelog sections. |"],[266,"| Module        | Responsibility                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `packages.ts` | `resolveI18nPackages()`, the config adapter that selects a directory, one package or all packages and resolves their options and POT paths. |"],[268,"| Module        | Responsibility                                                                                                        |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `generate.ts` | `extractCatalog()` and `generatePackagePot()`, which read the selected files of a package and write or check its POT. |"],[264,"| Module        | Responsibility                        |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `versions.ts` | Version checks and SemVer precedence. |"],[269,"| Module       | Responsibility                                                                                                                |\n| ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `compile.ts` | `compileTranslations()`, which compiles a translated PO into MO, script JSON or `.l10n.php` and writes or checks the outputs. |"],[267,"| Module       | Responsibility                                                              |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `options.ts` | Validating and merging the `i18n` options of the config layers and the CLI. |"],[273,"| Module       | Responsibility                                                   |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `catalog.ts` | Catalog types, merging messages of a domain and the diagnostics. |"],[270,"| Module     | Responsibility                                                                                           |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `files.ts` | Selecting source files with the include and exclude globs, independently of the distribution file rules. |"],[275,"| Module   | Responsibility                  |\n| -------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `pot.ts` | Serializing a catalog as a POT. |"],[168,"| Option                    | Description                                                                                                                                  |\n| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--exclude-gitattributes` | Also exclude paths marked `export-ignore` by Git attributes. This is opt-in and requires the source directory to be inside a Git repository. |"],[167,"| Option                 | Description                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--exclude <patterns>` | Extra gitignore-style patterns, comma-separated or repeated, like `--exclude build/,assets/*,images/**/*`. They're applied after the ignore file, so a `!` pattern can also re-include a file. |"],[165,"| Option                | Description                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `--distignore <file>` | Path to the ignore file. Defaults to `.distignore` in the source directory when present; an explicitly passed file must exist. |"],[166,"| Option            | Description                 |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `--no-distignore` | Don't read any ignore file. |"],[38,"| Placeholder | Value                                                                 | `plugins/alpha` | Root package |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- | ------------ |\n| `{path}`    | Package directory relative to `root`, empty for a package at the root | `plugins/alpha` | (empty)      |"],[39,"| Placeholder | Value              | `plugins/alpha`                                     | Root package                             |\n| ----------- | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `{name}`    | Package identifier | `plugins/alpha`, or its manifest name with `idFrom` | the manifest name, or the directory name |"],[40,"| Placeholder | Value            | `plugins/alpha` | Root package |\n| ----------- | ---------------- | --------------- | ------------ |\n| `{version}` | Released version | `1.2.0`         | `1.2.0`      |"],[26,"| Shared option                                  | Config equivalent                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--readme-changelog` / `--no-readme-changelog` | `readmeChangelog`, independent of updating the readme Stable tag |"],[27,"| Shared option                       | Config equivalent                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--changelog-insert-after <marker>` | `changelogInsertAfter`; must exist in `CHANGELOG.md` and, with readme changelog updates, the plugin readme |"],[23,"| Shared option            | Config equivalent                                                 |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--base-branch <branch>` | `baseBranch`, used with the remote for the default comparison ref |"],[25,"| Shared option            | Config equivalent                             |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `--changesets-dir <dir>` | `changesets`, relative to the repository root |"],[22,"| Shared option           | Config equivalent                                                                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--project-name <name>` | `id`, the literal changeset identifier of the root package, instead of the manifest name or checkout directory basename |"],[28,"| Shared option           | Config equivalent                               |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `--tag-format <format>` | `tagFormat`, replacing the default `v{version}` |"],[24,"| Shared option     | Config equivalent                                       |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--remote <name>` | `remote`, used for comparisons and pushing release tags |"],[121,"| Tool                                                             | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 10up WordPress.org plugin deploy and readme/asset update actions | Two separate GitHub Actions configured through env vars. A deploy always syncs the build to `trunk/` and copies it to the tag; readme and asset updates need the second action. Assets come from `.wordpress-org` by default, and files are selected with `.distignore` or `.gitattributes`. Files are synced with `rsync`. | One `deploy-svn` command with independent write flags, so a release, a readme-only update and an assets-only update are the same command with different flags. Runs locally and in any CI, not only GitHub Actions, and syncs in Node.js without `rsync`. Checks that the `Version` header, readme `Stable tag` and SVN tag agree before writing, which the 10up actions don't. Assets are deployed only when `--assets-dir` is passed, instead of always syncing `.wordpress-org` when it exists, so a plugin's own `assets/` folder is never uploaded by accident. Adds `--exclude` for patterns without an ignore file, opt-in Git `export-ignore` support and `--from-zip` to deploy the exact files of a ZIP. |"],[126,"| Tool                                          | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                          | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp i18n make-mo`, `make-json` and `make-php` | WP-CLI commands that compile PO files into MO, script JSON and `.l10n.php` files. `make-json` maps sources with `--use-map` and removes JavaScript strings from the PO unless `--no-purge` is passed. | `i18n-mo`, `i18n-json` and `i18n-php` compile one translated PO at a time, never change it, and leave out fuzzy, obsolete and untranslated entries. `i18n-json` takes a many-to-many `--map` from source files to built scripts, and JSX and TypeScript sources need one, so catalogs are never written for files WordPress doesn't load. `--check` compares the outputs without writing. There's no `update-po`, so update PO files from the POT with gettext's `msgmerge` or a translation platform. |"],[127,"| Tool                         | What it does                                                                          | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp plugin verify-checksums` | WP-CLI command that checks installed plugins against the WordPress.org checksums API. | Reads the same manifest format, but from a file, so it also works for plugins and themes that aren't hosted on WordPress.org and for build directories in CI. `checksums` writes the manifest that WordPress.org generates for its own plugins. |"],[128,"| Tool                    | What it does                                                                                                                         | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp-scripts plugin-zip` | Zips files from `package.json` `files` or a fixed allowlist, named after the npm package, with `--root-folder` / `--no-root-folder`. | Reuses the root folder flags, selects files with `.distignore` by default and can optionally honor Git `export-ignore` attributes, puts the version from the plugin headers in the filename and streams the archive instead of building it in memory. |"],[123,"| Tool                  | What it does                                                                     | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| codeinwp theme deploy | Fork of the 10up plugin action that commits themes to the themes SVN repository. | Doesn't deploy themes. Theme releases are ZIP uploads on WordPress.org; `zip` builds the upload with the theme folder as the root. |"],[125,"| Tool               | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                        | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `wp i18n make-pot` | WP-CLI command that extracts PHP, JavaScript, `block.json` and `theme.json` strings into a POT, with `--domain`, `--include`/`--exclude`, `--headers`, `--file-comment`, `--merge`/`--subtract` and audit warnings. | `i18n-pot` and `i18n-audit` need only Node.js, write byte-for-byte reproducible POTs without dates, and `--check` fails CI on a stale POT. Headers are set one at a time with a repeatable `--header NAME=VALUE` instead of a JSON object, and `--include`/`--exclude` take one glob each, so patterns never need escaping or comma splitting. `--no-references` and `--all-domains` say what they do. Monorepos keep these settings once in `wp-release.config.js` and select packages with `--package` or `--all` instead of running the command for every directory. The audit is a separate read-only command with stable finding codes for `--suppress`, instead of warnings mixed into generation. There's no `--merge`, `--subtract` or `--skip-*`; `--include` and `--exclude` cover the same needs. |"],[122,"| Tool              | What it does                                                                                                     | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grunt-wp-deploy` | Grunt task with `deploy_trunk` and `deploy_tag` booleans, interactive password prompts and a `Stable tag` check. | No Grunt dependency and never interactive. `--readme-only` is a separate flag, where `grunt-wp-deploy` can only update trunk with the full build. Assets are independent of trunk writes. |"],[124,"| Tool              | What it does                                                                                             | How `wp-release` differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wp dist-archive` | WP-CLI command that zips a project using `.distignore`, with `--plugin-dirname` and `--filename-format`. | Same `.distignore` file and `{name}.{version}` style filename format, but needs only Node.js instead of PHP and WP-CLI. `.distignore` is matched with full gitignore syntax, including `!` negation, and `--distignore` / `--exclude` let you point at another ignore file or add patterns on the command line. Builds are reproducible, and `--checksums` writes a manifest next to the ZIP. |"],[308,"A `translators:` comment attaches to the first translation call whose containing expression or nearest statement immediately follows it. Whitespace, parsed comments and ternary branch punctuation may intervene, but unrelated statements or argument expressions may not. Comments before outer wrappers reach nested translation calls; a comment is consumed once, so unrelated sibling calls do not inherit it. Multiline comment content is preserved. WordPress plugin/theme header labels serialize as extracted comments. Bundled metadata tables follow WordPress 6.8 block fields and theme fields, including styles, variations, presets, template names and nested block settings. Their upstream data license is GPL-2.0-or-later; tables are maintained intentionally with fixtures and never fetched during generation."],[332,"A `ValidationError` can list `problems`, each with a `message` and an optional `file`, `line` and `title`. `logError( logger, error, title )` logs each problem as an error on its file and line and lists them in the summary under the title, or logs the message when there are none. `VersionMismatchError` and `version-check` report the version mismatches this way."],[397,"A checksums manifest proves only that the files match the manifest. With `--public-key <key>`, `verify` first checks `<checksums>.sig` against the Ed25519 public key and fails with exit code `2` before comparing any file when the signature is missing or invalid, so that the manifest itself can be trusted. `--signature-format minisign` or `ssh` checks `<checksums>.minisig` or `<checksums>.sshsig` instead. The key can be the base64 key, the minisign public key or the `ssh-ed25519` public key, since they're forms of the same key. See Sign."],[225,"A deploy releases the files of the working tree, so build the packages first. One job that builds once and runs `deploy-git --release-ref \"$GITHUB_REF\"` is the simplest setup, also for a branch that releases every package. A matrix with a job for each package from `--list` would build every package in every job, so build once and pass the built tree to the jobs as an artifact if you need one, for example for separate credentials."],[117,"A failed command adds its error to the summary, or a table of its problems with their files and lines, listing up to 100 and counting the rest. The Markdown of the summaries isn't part of the library API. File paths are relative to `GITHUB_WORKSPACE`, or the current directory without it. Files outside it, like those of an extracted ZIP, get an annotation without a file. Multi-line messages stay one annotation."],[391,"A file matches when its SHA-256 equals the manifest. Manifests without SHA-256 fall back to MD5, and a hash can also be a list of accepted values."],[396,"A manifest can't list itself. A copy of the manifest and of its signature inside the checked files, at the root with the same names as the `--checksums` file or at the path of `--embedded`, is skipped when comparing files, but it must be identical to the `--checksums` file and its signature. The signature is the `.sig` file, or the file of `--signature-format`. A path that the manifest lists is never skipped, and a file that isn't a matching copy is reported as not in the checksums. See Manifest inside the plugin."],[68,"A pre-release writes every version target except the readme `Stable tag`, which keeps the last stable version since WordPress.org offers it to every site, and `changeset-tag` skips the `Stable tag` when it verifies a pre-release. The changelog gets a section for the pre-release, like `## 1.3.0-rc.1 (2026-10-12)`, so that `changeset-tag` finds its release notes, and the changesets are consumed like in any release."],[312,"A script map is a JSON object from normalized package-relative source references to arrays of normalized runtime script paths. Many source files can feed one bundle, and one source can feed several scripts. An explicit map selects only listed references; an empty target array excludes that source. Without a map, JS/MJS/CJS references use identity paths, while JSX/TypeScript references require mapping. PHP-only references do not enter JSON catalogs."],[11,"A single configured package is selected automatically for `changeset-add --bump` when `--since` isn't supplied, and for `changeset-tag --version`; `--since` still selects changed packages. npm and Composer projects use their root manifest's name as the changeset identifier when that manifest supplies the version; other projects use the root directory's basename. `--project-name <name>` pins the identifier of a repository without a manifest name, so local checkouts and CI don't need matching directory names; a config sets the same identifier with `id`. The fallback uses `.changeset/`, `CHANGELOG.md`, tags named `v{version}`, and the `origin` remote. There is no default comparison branch: pass `--since` or `--base-branch` when status checks should compare changes. Readme changelog updates are opt-in."],[198,"A version mismatch throws a `VersionMismatchError`, a `ValidationError` with the `mismatches` and a problem for each, with its file and line. See Logging."],[331,"A writer implements `LogWriter` with `write()`, `startGroup()`, `endGroup()` and `summary()`, so other CI systems can have their own format. `summary()` takes a function that builds the Markdown, which only writers with a summary call."],[49,"Add release notes in a feature PR with `npx wp-release changeset-add`. In a terminal, it lists the packages, marks the ones that changed since `<remote>/<baseBranch>` (or `--since <ref>`) without a changeset, and asks for the packages to release, a `patch`, `minor` or `major` bump for each, and the release note. Choose packages by number, identifier or path, or press Enter to release the changed ones; `none` adds an empty changeset. Changed files include uncommitted and untracked ones, since changesets are usually added before committing."],[63,"After merging the release PR, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --push` pushes tags only for packages changed by that release. It pushes one tag at a time because GitHub doesn't trigger workflows for a push of more than three tags. wp-release doesn't create GitHub Releases: with `--json` or `--github-output`, each entry of `tags` has the `tag`, a `title` like `plugins/alpha@1.2.0` or, for a package at the repository root, the release title like `1.2.0` (see releaseTitle, and `--release-title` for overriding it in one command), the release `notes` and a `prerelease` flag, for a workflow step like `softprops/action-gh-release` to create the release (see the example below). For initial adoption, preview all missing tags with `changeset-tag --dry-run` (without `--since`), then push if needed. To release each package with a separate workflow instead, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --untagged --github-output` lists the releases that don't have a tag yet, without creating any. Tag pushes need Git write credentials."],[60,"After versioning, run `npm install --package-lock-only` so that the root lockfile resolves the rewritten pins to the workspace links again; the `Prepare Release` workflow (`prepare-release.yml` in this monorepo) does it with its release pull request."],[412,"All formats are Ed25519 signatures by the same key, so one key and one secret cover all of them. The tests check each format with `minisign`, `ssh-keygen` and libsodium, and check the signatures that `minisign` and `ssh-keygen` write with `wp-release verify`."],[31,"An empty changeset marks a feature PR as no-release. The version workflow consumes it without changing versions, and a release PR containing only its deletion produces no tags."],[404,"An existing signature file isn't overwritten without `--force`."],[363,"An existing ZIP or manifest isn't overwritten without `--force`. A ZIP or manifest written inside the source directory is never archived."],[29,"An explicit `--config` must load successfully. Otherwise a discovered config takes precedence over the single-project fallback. Single-project descriptor and settings flags cannot be mixed with a config file; put those settings in the config instead. The existing global `--tag-format` override works in both modes. Config files remain the place for package layouts, dependents, custom version and changelog callbacks, deployment destinations and distribution settings. Packaging and deployment commands retain their existing config requirements."],[229,"Architecture"],[189,"Asset images get `svn:mime-type` set for PNG, JPEG, GIF and SVG, the formats that WordPress.org documents for plugin assets. Other formats keep the type that svn assigns."],[375,"Because the manifest lists the files and not the ZIP, it stays valid for any copy of the same files: the GitHub release ZIP, the ZIP that WordPress.org builds from SVN, or an installed plugin on a site."],[339,"Build a directory"],[279,"Build and release WordPress plugins, themes, and packages."],[289,"Changeset releases"],[326,"Changeset releases:"],[1,"Changesets"],[2,"Changesets support"],[342,"Checksums"],[334,"Checksums:"],[72,"CLI"],[73,"CLI"],[288,"CLI and automation"],[161,"CLI JSON results use camelCase keys like `zipPath`, and GitHub Action outputs use kebab-case like `zip-path`. The ZIP action supports `package` and `config`, the checksums action supports `config` for codec selection, and the SVN action supports `release-ref`, repeated `package` values as newline-separated input, `list`, `config` and `tag-format`."],[120,"Comparison with existing tools"],[296,"Comparison with existing tools"],[119,"Comparison with other tools"],[300,"Compile translated catalogs"],[302,"Config-free source directories must have plugin/theme headers. The actual main file is discovered using the package metadata reader, including plugins whose main filename differs from their slug. The selected domain defaults to `Text Domain`, then the directory name. Calls with omitted domains belong to WordPress's `default` domain; block metadata without a domain inherits the package domain. `--all-domains` explicitly combines domains. A block's explicit `textdomain` always controls its messages."],[129,"Configuration"],[345,"Configuration and custom codecs"],[290,"Configuration and file selection"],[53,"Configure `changelogEntry( { package, changeset, commit } )` to format the changelog entry of each changeset, where `commit` is the `{ hash, shortHash, subject }` of the commit that added the changeset file, if it's committed. The formatted entries go into `CHANGELOG.md`, the `changelog` of each release in the `changeset-version` result, and so the release notes; plugin readmes keep the plain summaries. Without the callback, entries are the plain summaries. For example, to link each entry to its commit:"],[54,"Configure `dependents` to re-release packages that bundle a package when it releases. The dependents come from what the packages declare: a package is a dependent of a source when the spec that it declares in its npm `dependencies` or `optionalDependencies` (or the sections that the policy adds) resolves the current version of the source or points at the workspace, and it re-releases when the next version of the source falls outside the declared range, or always with `when: 'always'`. A pin to an older version selects a registry release instead and `changeset-status` warns about the drift until the pin resolves the package again. Versioning a dependent rewrites its declared specs of the source to the released version, keeping their range prefixes, so an exact pin matches the released workspace again and the built bundle matches the changelog. Wildcards and unpinned ranges accept the release without one, like the `devDependencies` of a package. The derived dependents appear in `changeset-status`, `changeset-version` and `changeset-tag` like any other release, and a dependent that releases for its own reasons keeps its own bump and appends the bundle note:"],[240,"Configured repositories use the layout and naming conventions their config sets. Config-free changeset releases use the root-only detection and defaults described above."],[247,"Constructors only store their options. Work starts in a method call."],[192,"Credentials are only needed for the commit, not for dry runs, no-op runs or `file://` repositories. Pass the username with `--svn-username` or `SVN_USERNAME` and the password in `SVN_PASSWORD`. svn always runs non-interactively without caching credentials, and the password is passed on the standard input with `--password-from-stdin`, never on the command line or in the logs."],[175,"Deploy configured packages"],[41,"Deploy templates also have `{branch}`, `{ref}` and `{revision}` (see Deploy configured packages), and any template can be a callback that receives the package, for names that the placeholders don't cover, like the last directory of the path."],[174,"Deploy to Git"],[173,"Deploy to SVN"],[172,"Deployment"],[227,"Development"],[228,"Development"],[295,"Development and architecture"],[105,"Each command also adds a section to the job summary of the run, when `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` is set:"],[359,"Entries are sorted and use a fixed timestamp and file mode. Built-in metadata is deterministic except for embedded minisign signatures, which include their signing timestamp; custom codec callbacks control their own encoded bytes."],[102,"Errors, warnings and notices are printed as workflow commands, so they show up as annotations on the run and on the files of a pull request:"],[55,"Excluded packages still release with their own changesets and stay out of the automatic re-releases."],[316,"Exit codes"],[12,"Explicit descriptors replace automatic detection and define the entire version target list. For a plugin whose version appears in its header, readme and a PHP constant:"],[21,"File descriptor flags are repeatable. The source is inferred from a sole descriptor, or a unique descriptor other than a Stable tag. Otherwise use `--version-from <kind>`, where kind is `package-json`, `composer-json`, `wordpress-plugin`, `wordpress-theme`, `wordpress-stable-tag`, `php-constant` or `git-tag`. For repeated kinds, qualify the selector with the exact descriptor value: `--version-from package-json:package.json` or `--version-from 'php-constant:my-plugin.php#MY_PLUGIN_VERSION'`. Argument order doesn't select the source. A Git source requires a matching existing stable tag; its pattern selects the tags to read, while `--tag-format` controls tags to create. With multiple descriptors, explicitly select `git-tag` if it is the authoritative source."],[131,"File selection"],[343,"Filenames, embedding and signing"],[384,"For a manifest that is also published as a separate release asset or deployed directly from a source directory, write and sign it in that directory before packaging. Use `zip --embed-checksums` as described above when only the archive should contain the generated metadata. The source-directory workflow uses the same manifest for installed copies and release assets:"],[34,"For a plugin with several version targets, put the same descriptor flags in npm scripts for all four changeset commands. Full Git history and tags are needed for comparisons, Git version sources and release notes. Keep the checkout directory name consistent when it supplies the changeset identifier. Directory artifacts use `npx --no-install wp-release build --output dist/my-plugin --github-output`; upload that directory with your artifact action. `build` writes manifest sidecars outside the directory and does not accept the ZIP-only embedding, codec or manifest-filename flags."],[10,"For a root npm package, the complete workflow needs no project flags:"],[66,"For local development, run the TypeScript source with `npm run wp-release -- <command>` (for example, `npm run wp-release -- changeset-status --since origin/main`). GitHub workflows use the same CLI for versioning and tagging. `SUBTREE_DEPLOY_TOKEN` lets them update the release PR and push tags, and the `Tag Release` workflow passes it to `softprops/action-gh-release` to create the GitHub Releases."],[344,"Formats"],[413,"Generate a key pair with `wp-release keygen`. It prints the private seed once as `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY=…` and the matching public key, also as a minisign public key and an `ssh-ed25519` public key. Generate keys locally and store the private seed in your secret manager or CI secrets. Distribute the public key to verifiers. `keygen --github-output` is rejected to keep the private seed out of workflow outputs; regular and `--json` output both include the seed."],[292,"Git and WordPress.org deployment"],[324,"Git deploys:"],[74,"GitHub Actions annotations and job summaries"],[282,"Guides"],[184,"How a deploy works:"],[202,"How a deploy works:"],[51,"In CI, `changeset-status --since origin/main` requires a changeset added by the PR; changesets already on the base do not count, while an empty PR changeset records an explicit no-release decision. Missing-change annotations identify an affected file."],[96,"In GitHub Actions, `--github-output` writes the complete result as JSON to `steps.<id>.outputs.result` and exposes each top-level string, number or boolean as a plain output. Use `steps.zip.outputs.zipPath` for a ZIP path, for example. Arrays and objects remain in `result`: tag metadata is read with `fromJSON( steps.tag.outputs.result ).tags[0].tag`, even when only one package is selected. There is no plain `steps.tag.outputs.tag` output."],[354,"In the Deploy workflow, a directory build can be deployed with `wp-release deploy-svn dist/code-prettify --slug code-prettify --trunk --svn-tag \"$GITHUB_REF_NAME\" --no-distignore`. Keep `--from-zip` when the SVN deployment must match the attached ZIP exactly. Run dependency installation and compilation before `build`; it only selects and copies files."],[280,"Install"],[297,"Internationalization"],[309,"Invalid syntax, unreadable selected files, selected source symlinks, empty/reserved message IDs, conflicting plural definitions and output errors fail generation. Unsupported dynamic arguments warn and are fatal with `--strict`. Audit adds `placeholder-comment`, `unordered-placeholders`, `plural-placeholders`, `empty-message` and `conflicting-comments` findings; suppress these explicitly by code where justified. Placeholders support `%%`, positional indexes, numeric width/precision, padding/flags and PHP/JS sprintf conversion types. Audit checks extraction and message quality, not every untranslated user-facing string. Audit fails on errors; strict audit also fails on warnings. Failed checks return exit code `2`; unexpected I/O/option-loading errors follow the existing CLI error handling. `--json`, GitHub annotations, summaries and step outputs use the existing CLI reporting facilities."],[307,"JavaScript extraction uses Babel's parser and lexical scope traversal, without transforms or project Babel config. JS, JSX, TS, TSX, MJS, CJS, MTS and CTS are supported. Named imports/aliases and namespace imports from `@wordpress/i18n`, unshadowed `wp.i18n` calls and unbound canonical legacy `__`, `_x`, `_n` and `_nx` calls extract strings. `--no-legacy-globals` requires imported or `wp.i18n` bindings. Locally shadowed/reassigned imports are excluded. Static literals, templates without substitutions, concatenation and TypeScript assertions are supported. Destructured CommonJS imports, transpiled `(0, i18n.__)` forms and arbitrary minified bundles are outside the supported syntax; scan source and use explicit source/build mapping for compiled JSON."],[313,"JSON filenames are `<domain>-<Language>-<md5(runtime-path)>.json`; the WordPress `default` domain uses `<Language>-<md5(runtime-path)>.json`. WordPress's `.min.js` normalization applies consistently to grouping, hashing and the JSON `source`. Jed keys preserve context through `\\u0004`, plural arrays contain all translated forms, and revision dates come from the PO metadata. `.l10n.php` writes safely escaped data arrays with NUL-separated plural translations; catalog text is never treated as executable PHP."],[317,"Library"],[294,"Library API"],[315,"Library API"],[163,"Library classes are named after the capability (`SvnDeploy`, `Svn`, `GitDeploy`) without repeating \"release\"."],[318,"Logging"],[346,"Manifest inside the plugin"],[393,"Manifest paths outside the package folder (absolute or with `..`) are rejected."],[311,"MO and `.l10n.php` default beside the PO, with `.mo` and `.l10n.php` extensions; `--output` selects an explicit file relative to cwd. WordPress PHP catalogs require WordPress 6.5 or later. JSON requires an explicit domain; `--output` is a directory, defaulting to the PO directory. `--check` compares proposed bytes without writing and exits `2` on absent/stale outputs. Compiler commands are primitives and do not select configured packages."],[130,"Naming convention"],[286,"Node.js 22.12 or later is required. SVN deployments also require the `svn` command line client. Git deployments and changeset commands require Git."],[52,"On the release branch, preview with `changeset-version --dry-run`, then run `changeset-version` to update version targets and changelogs. By default, new Markdown entries go before the first version heading; `changelogInsertAfter` sets a literal insertion marker for `CHANGELOG.md` and, with `readmeChangelog`, the plugin readme. Put the marker in both files; a missing marker is an error. Without a marker, plugin readmes use the `== Changelog ==` heading. Failed updates are rolled back for safe retries."],[226,"Other monorepo tools split the same way with less of the release in the config: splitsh-lite, Symplify MonorepoBuilder and `danharrin/monorepo-split-github-action` push the source history of each package directory, with the packages and repositories listed in the workflow or a matrix, while `deploy-git` releases the built files on top of that history, so `vendor/` and `build/` reach Composer and WordPress installs. Like the publishers of GoReleaser, targets are lists with templates, and pre-releases skip the targets where they would replace the stable version."],[5,"Packages and tags"],[338,"Packaging and integrity"],[291,"Packaging, checksums, signatures, and verification"],[337,"Parsers return messages and structured diagnostics with stable codes, severity and source-relative locations. Catalog messages retain IDs, contexts, plurals, translations, translator comments, flags and structured references. `aggregateCatalog()` selects/merges a domain, unions references/comments and diagnoses plural conflicts. `resolveI18nPackages()` is the separate config adapter; extraction and serialization primitives know nothing about the repository. Library functions do not log, parse process arguments or set exit codes. Extraction results report unique files consulted, including the main plugin/theme file used for headers and header strings even when source globs exclude it. `parseTranslatedPo()`, `compileMo()`, `compileScriptTranslations()`, `compilePhpTranslations()` and `compileTranslations()` expose the same typed operations to library callers."],[101,"Pass `--log-format text` for plain text in GitHub Actions. The GitHub Actions always use the `github` log format. Only the CLI checks `GITHUB_ACTIONS`: the library classes don't read the environment, and choose the format by the writer they get."],[306,"PHP extraction uses `php-parser` in PHP 8.3 mode, tested with attributes, enums, match expressions, promoted properties and readonly classes. Supported WordPress functions are `__`, `_e`, `_x`, `_ex`, `_n`, `_nx`, `_n_noop`, `_nx_noop`, `esc_attr__`, `esc_attr_e`, `esc_attr_x`, `esc_html__`, `esc_html_e` and `esc_html_x`. Global and explicitly global calls, namespace fallback and explicit function imports/aliases are supported. Known file-local declarations and unrelated namespaced imports are excluded; methods and static methods are never translation calls. Named arguments resolve by PHP parameter name. Literal strings, static concatenation and non-interpolated heredoc/nowdoc preserve decoded escapes, Unicode and newlines. Counts may be dynamic; IDs, contexts and selected domains must be static."],[132,"Plugin and theme headers"],[196,"Pre-release versions like `1.0.0-rc.1` can be tagged, but syncing one to `trunk/` warns, since trunk is for the development version."],[6,"Pre-releases"],[304,"Precedence is defaults, root configuration, package group/individual entry, then explicit command options. Scalars override, lists replace inherited lists, and gettext headers merge by key, case-insensitively, preserving intentionally empty values. A header set to `false` is removed, like `'Project-Id-Version': false` to keep the plugin version out of the POT so that it changes only when strings change; a later layer can set it again. `Content-Type` can't be removed, since it declares the UTF-8 charset. Options include `enabled`, `domain`, `allDomains`, `include`, `exclude`, `destination`, `headers`, `fileComment`, `references`, `strict`, `legacyGlobals` and audit `suppress`. `fileComment` adds static text above the POT header; each line is emitted as a gettext comment, and no copyright year is generated. The CLI accepts the same setting with `--file-comment`. Repeat `--include` or `--exclude` for multiple globs; these use case-sensitive minimatch semantics with `/` paths, `**`, brace expansion and dotfile matching, without gitignore negation or comma splitting. Default excludes cover dependency directories, dot directories, tests, minified files and generated translations. Source/build exclusions are a repository choice; this repository excludes `build`, `tools` and isolated vendors in its config."],[97,"Prefer plain outputs in steps that run conditionally, since a skipped step has empty outputs. When reading JSON from a step that may be skipped, use a fallback such as `fromJSON( steps.tag.outputs.result || '{\"tags\":[]}' ).tags[0].tag`. For several tags or deploys, read the arrays in `result` to build a job matrix or process each item."],[95,"Run `npx wp-release <command> --help` for the options of each command. Directory-based commands accept a first `<source-dir>` argument or `--source-dir`, defaulting to the current directory. `sign` and the `i18n-mo`, `i18n-json` and `i18n-php` compilers take file arguments, `keygen` generates keys, and changeset commands operate on the configured repository. Every command prints its result as JSON with `--json`. Commands that take no positional arguments, like the changeset commands, reject bare arguments with the usage instead of ignoring them."],[310,"Runtime formats require a translated PO, not an untranslated POT. PO updating, fuzzy matching and automatic translation are not provided. Compilation validates `Language` and `Plural-Forms`, excludes fuzzy, obsolete and untranslated entries, preserves contexts and plural forms, and never changes the input PO. An incompletely translated plural entry is excluded as a unit. Gettext parsing handles source encoding; runtime outputs use UTF-8. Each output is replaced atomically; JSON outputs are independent writes, and obsolete output files are not deleted automatically."],[366,"See Checksums for filenames, placement, formats and configuration."],[348,"Sign"],[349,"Signature formats"],[341,"Signing targets for builds and ZIPs"],[69,"Since a pre-release consumes the changesets that the stable release needs, run it on a branch that isn't merged, and tag that branch commit:"],[4,"Single-project checks in GitHub Actions"],[3,"Single-project repositories without a config file"],[7,"Snapshots"],[281,"Start with the CLI"],[299,"Supported extraction syntax"],[9,"The `changeset-*` commands can release one project at the repository root without a `wp-release.config.*` file. Run them from the root directory. With no version descriptor flags, they detect a root `package.json` or `composer.json` with a version, a plugin entry PHP file with a `Plugin Name` header, or a theme `style.css` with a `Theme Name` header. A detected plugin also updates the Stable tag in `readme.txt`, or `readme.md` when no Stable tag is found in `readme.txt`. Multiple version sources, multiple plugin entry files and npm workspaces require explicit descriptors or a config; files belonging to different projects aren't silently synchronized. A directory without a recognized version source still requires a config or explicit descriptors."],[62,"The `changeset-version` result lists each release with its `package`, `bump`, `currentVersion`, `nextVersion` and `changelog` entries, a Markdown list like the one added to `CHANGELOG.md`. Use it to name and describe the release pull request in a repository script, or call `versionChangesets()` from the library and format the entries of each release with `formatChangelogEntries( release.changesets )`."],[59,"The `dependents.dependency` hooks extend the built-in npm scan instead of replacing it, so that both work behind the same range rule and rewrite:"],[372,"The `wp-release-checksums` GitHub Action runs this command."],[176,"The `wp-release-deploy-svn` GitHub Action runs this command."],[399,"The `wp-release-sign` GitHub Action runs this command."],[355,"The `wp-release-zip` GitHub Action runs this command."],[284,"The `wp-release-zip`, `wp-release-checksums`, `wp-release-sign`, and `wp-release-deploy-svn` GitHub Actions expose the same commands in workflows."],[235,"The `wpelevator-source` export condition points at `src/index.ts`. The wp-release tests and monorepo CLI scripts use it to work with the source instead of a `dist/` build. Action builds use the declared dependency's default export, which resolves to `dist/` for the workspace copy and the published package. Build wp-release before building the actions."],[70,"The base branch keeps its stable versions and pending changesets, so the stable release later versions them as usual, in one changelog section. The tag keeps the pre-release commit reachable after the branch is reset or deleted. The release notes of a stable release compare with the previous stable release, skipping the pre-releases before it, and the `git-tag` version source ignores pre-release tags, so packages versioned by git tags keep building on their last stable version."],[57,"The bundle changelog entry defaults to `Bundle <source> <version>.` and is formatted with `dependents.changelog( { package, source } )`:"],[8,"The changeset commands manage versioning, changelogs and release tags for single-package repositories and monorepos, including this repository. They read Changesets-style Markdown and load JavaScript configs with descriptors for npm and Composer manifests, plugin and theme headers, readme Stable tags, PHP constants, and Git tags. Use JavaScript configs with ESM (`export default`) or CommonJS (`module.exports`). Frontmatter uses `package-name: patch|minor|major` entries. For pre-releases, use the built-in pre-release workflow; the parser supports this simple frontmatter format rather than general YAML or the Changesets pre mode."],[248,"The changeset release modules, from the entry point down:"],[170,"The commands read the same headers as WordPress. The main plugin file is the top-level `.php` file with a `Plugin Name:` header in its first 8 KB, preferring `{slug}.php` when several files have one. A directory without a plugin file and with `Theme Name:` in `style.css` is a theme. The readme headers (`Stable tag`, `Tested up to`, …) come from `readme.txt`, or `readme.md` when there's no `readme.txt`."],[35,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[305,"The default output is `<Domain Path>/<text-domain>.pot`, or `languages/<text-domain>.pot`. A leading `/` in `Domain Path` is package-relative, and configured destinations cannot escape the package. `--destination` changes the package-relative directory. An explicit `--output <file.pot>` resolves against the command's working directory and requires one package. All multi-package destinations are validated before writing; each completed package replaces its file atomically, independently of other packages. `--check` compares bytes without writing and fails if the POT is absent or stale. Source references are preserved by default; `--no-references` omits them. `POT-Creation-Date` and `PO-Revision-Date` default to empty values; no current date or year is inserted. Name/version headers come from metadata, and `--header NAME=VALUE` overrides any default, including with an empty value, and `--header NAME` removes the header. The POT lists the plugin or theme header strings first, then the strings of PHP, JavaScript, `block.json` and other metadata files in source order, so related strings stay together for translators. Each entry has its extracted comments, references and flags before the strings, in the usual gettext layout. The default headers come in a fixed order, and custom headers follow them. No generator header or generated copyright comment is added; set `fileComment` for a static one."],[47,"The default strategy bumps stable `x.y.z` versions with SemVer rules, and the `x.y` and `x.y.z.w` versions common in WordPress plugins the same way: the bumped part is raised, the parts after it reset to `0`, and the version keeps its number of parts. A minor bump of `2.1` is `2.2` and a major bump is `3.0`, while a patch bump adds the third part, `2.1.1`. A patch bump of `1.2.3.4` is `1.2.4.0`, so raising only the fourth part needs a custom strategy. Configure `nextVersion( { package, bump, currentVersion, changesets } )` for other formats; changeset bumps remain `patch`, `minor` or `major`. Pre-releases are added to the next version with `--prerelease`, described below."],[378,"The default WordPress filename remains the ZIP basename with `.checksums.json` instead of `.zip`, in either placement mode. Choose `--checksums-file checksums.json` for a stable name. The version remains in the JSON content."],[352,"The destination is required and automatically excluded when it is inside the source. It cannot be the source directory or an ancestor, including through symlinked parents. Existing destinations require `--force`, which replaces the directory and removes stale files; existing files and symbolic links are rejected. Files are copied to a temporary directory before the existing build is replaced. Selected symbolic links are rejected as with ZIP packaging. `--json` returns `outputDir`, `files` and, for configured builds, `package`; it also returns `checksumsPath` when a manifest is generated, and `signatures` and `publicKey` when signing is requested. `--github-output` exposes these results too."],[415,"The expected version is `--version`, or the `Version` header."],[188,"The files are synced in Node.js, without `rsync`: new and changed files are copied, files that are no longer selected are deleted, and `svn add` and `svn delete` stage them, including files that svn ignores by default. With `--trunk`, `trunk/` is then copied to the tag with `svn copy`. Without it, the same selected files are synced into `tags/<version>` instead, and with `--force` an existing tag is updated to match, deleting files that are no longer in the build."],[241,"The GitHub Actions are adapters to these commands: they use the same flag names and `execute()` entry point, with secrets supplied through environment variables. The SVN action injects a command runner that checks or installs SVN only when a deployment runs its first SVN command; config planning and deployment behavior remain in `wp-release`."],[234,"The i18n tests validate gettext round trips, script catalogs and generated PHP data without launching PHP, WP-CLI or WordPress, covering contexts, plural forms, comments, flags, Unicode, escaping and PHP-looking input as data."],[265,"The internationalization modules in `src/i18n/`, from the entry points down:"],[402,"The key is read from the `WP_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY` environment variable only, as a base64 32-byte Ed25519 seed or a 64-byte libsodium secret key, the formats that `sodium_crypto_sign_seed_keypair()` and the update-pilot-server package signing key take. There's no flag for it, since it would show in the process list, and it's never logged."],[388,"The manifest can have any name and location, for example `--output build/includes/signatures/checksums.json`, and missing folders are created. Tell `verify` where the shipped copy is with `--embedded <path>`, like `--embedded includes/signatures/checksums.json`. Both the `--checksums` file and its signature must then exist, both copies must be shipped, and they're skipped when comparing files and must be identical to the originals. The manifest can't list the embedded path. Without `--embedded`, `verify` looks for the manifest name at the root, and skips a file only when it exists, the matching original exists and the manifest doesn't list it. Otherwise it's checked like any other file, so a listed file that has the name of the manifest can't be deleted unnoticed, and a shipped `.sig` without an original is reported as not in the checksums."],[142,"The names below are scoped to their commands. `--output` selects the primary artifact of each command; ZIP packaging uses `--checksums-file` for its accompanying manifest:"],[381,"The optional top-level `integrity` configuration maps to these flags. Explicit CLI values override configuration; `--no-embed-checksums` and `--no-sign-checksums` disable their configured behaviors. Existing release configurations retain their `packages` array; use `packages: []` for a configuration containing only integrity settings."],[185,"The options are validated, including the slug, version and readme name, before they're used in URLs or paths."],[314,"The package can be installed and used in any WordPress plugin or theme project; repository package selection is optional, and directory mode works without a wp-release config."],[287,"The package is an ES module with TypeScript declarations. CommonJS projects can load it with `require( '@wpelevator/wp-release' )` on Node.js 22.12 or later."],[242,"The package uses ES modules as its unit of encapsulation, like most Node.js CLI tools: a module exports a few functions and keeps its helpers private, so there's no class to wrap them in. A class is used only when it holds state across steps or binds dependencies, so that callers and tests can replace them:"],[204,"The previous release is fetched: the tip of `--branch`, or for a tag-only deploy the tip of the repository's default branch (its `HEAD`), whatever that branch holds. Release a tag together with `--branch` to choose the branch it builds on."],[389,"The public key has to come from somewhere other than the plugin that it checks, because someone who can overwrite the plugin files can also replace a key and a signature inside them. A copy of the manifest also doesn't prevent a downgrade to an older release that was signed too, so the installing side has to enforce a minimum version."],[416,"The readme `Stable tag` must match for plugins; `--no-stable-tag` skips it, and themes have no Stable tag."],[208,"The release is pushed to the branch, unless it didn't change or is outdated, and to the tag, in one atomic push. Pushes are never forced, so the branch only moves forward and a tag is never replaced, also when it was created in the meantime."],[65,"The release notes are the version's `CHANGELOG.md` section, or without one the commits that changed the package since its previous tag, the highest version below the released one, skipping pre-releases for a stable version. Configure `releaseNotes( { package, version, tag, previousTag, changelog, commits, notes } )` to format them, for example to add links; `notes` is the default text and `commits` lists `{ hash, shortHash, subject }`, newest first. `changeset-tag --package <name> --dry-run` prints them without creating the tag."],[187,"The repository is checked out sparsely into a new temporary directory, with only the directories that the flags write to. A missing `assets/` (or `trunk/`, `tags/`) directory is created. The deploy stops when `tags/<version>` exists, unless `--force` replaces it."],[406,"The same key can also sign in formats that tools outside WordPress check, for people who download a release by hand or verify it in other pipelines. Pick them with `--format`, comma-separated or repeated; the default is `wordpress`:"],[13,"The same project flags belong on `changeset-add`, `changeset-status`, `changeset-version` and `changeset-tag`. Keep them consistent across all four commands, for example in command-specific npm scripts. An accompanying `package.json` used for build tools isn't changed unless it is included explicitly."],[203,"The selected files are written to the object database of the source repository and become a tree, with a temporary index, so the working tree and the index of the repository are left alone. Executable files stay executable."],[387,"The signature covers file hashes and not a ZIP, so it stays valid for the ZIP that WordPress.org builds from SVN. Check what WordPress.org serves after a deploy, with the manifest and public key from your build:"],[401,"The signature is the one WordPress core's `verify_file_signature()` checks: a base64 Ed25519 signature of the raw SHA-384 digest of the file. The tests check it against `sodium_crypto_sign_verify_detached()` in PHP. A `.sig` file can hold several signatures, one per line, and a file is valid when any of them match, which allows a key rotation window."],[230,"The source is TypeScript in `src/`, with tests next to the files they test (`*.test.ts`). The published package contains only the compiled `dist/` with JavaScript and `.d.ts` files; tests and translation fixtures stay in the repository."],[207,"The tag is checked in the repository first. A tag that already has the same files is the release: it's reused and nothing is pushed, so running a tag release again is a no-op. A tag with other files fails with exit code `2` before anything is pushed."],[395,"The version in the manifest must match the `Version` header of the checked files, so that an older release with its own valid manifest can't pass as a newer one."],[162,"The ZIP CLI result uses `checksumsEmbedded` for the package-relative embedded manifest path. Its `signatures` array includes external absolute paths and embedded package-relative paths, while `checksumsSignatures` contains only manifest signatures. The ZIP action exposes those locations as `checksums-embedded`, `signatures` for external signatures, and `signatures-embedded` for embedded signatures, so asset uploads receive local files. With CLI `--github-output`, signature arrays remain in the JSON `result` output."],[374,"Themes use a `theme` key instead of `plugin`, and the version is left out when it's unknown. The manifest is named `{slug}.{version}.checksums.json` after `--filename-format`, the same name as the ZIP with `.checksums.json` instead of `.zip`, so the two are published side by side; `--output <file>` writes it elsewhere. A manifest written inside `<source-dir>` is never listed in itself."],[33,"There are no dedicated build or changeset wrapper actions. Install the CLI in your project and run it directly. A root npm package can check a pull request with:"],[371,"These flags deliberately replace the former implicit signing behavior: `zip --checksums --sign` now leaves the manifest unsigned, and `--sign-format` alone is rejected. Add `--sign-checksums` to sign a manifest and `--sign` to sign a ZIP. The standalone `checksums` and `sign` commands remain available for workflows that write and sign a manifest inside the source before packaging it."],[365,"To embed a signed manifest with a stable filename, use:"],[64,"To release a single package, for example from a manual workflow, pass `--package <name>`, repeatable, and with one package `--version <version>` to fail unless the package is at that version. Packages versioned by git tags have no version file, so for them `--version` is the version to tag. Before a tag is created, every `version.to` target must match the version, so a plugin `Version` header that disagrees with its readme `Stable tag` blocks the tag. Versions with a pre-release part, like `1.0.0-rc.1`, have `prerelease` set, so their GitHub Releases can be marked as pre-releases. This example selects one package, so `tags[0]` is its release; these fields are nested in the JSON result:"],[209,"To release a tag again, for example after a release with the wrong files, delete the tag in the repository (`git push <url> :refs/tags/<tag>`) and run the deploy again. There's no flag for replacing a tag, since installs, Composer and GitHub Actions pins trust that a published tag doesn't change."],[61,"To release packages separately, for example with a release pull request for each, pass `--package <name>`, repeatable, to version only those packages. A changeset that also names other packages keeps only their entries and its summary, so it still applies to their releases with the same bump. Changesets without packages are left for a run without `--package`."],[336,"Translations:"],[30,"Typical release flow, from a feature pull request to the publish workflows triggered by release tags:"],[183,"Typical runs:"],[382,"Use `codec: 'sha256sum'`, `algorithms: ['sha256']`, `file: 'SHA256SUMS'` and `sign: { formats: ['minisign'] }` for the GNU setup. Custom codecs provide `id`, a default `filename`, and `encode`/`decode` callbacks, which may be asynchronous. Generation validates the round trip before writing artifacts: codecs must preserve all selected file paths, represented digests and any represented package identity. They receive and return the existing WordPress-compatible `ChecksumsManifest` model (`plugin` or `theme`, optional `version`, and a `files` map of relative paths to digest fields), keeping format customization separate from selection, hashing and signing."],[118,"Use global `--config <path>` to select a config; relative paths resolve from the current directory. Otherwise, commands using the config discover `wp-release.config.js`, `.mjs` or `.cjs` at the repository root. Global `--tag-format <format>` replaces the `tagFormat` of the config for one command, see Packages and tags."],[193,"Validation:"],[398,"Verification never executes configuration from inside a downloaded package. Without `--checksums`, `verify` reads the embedded filename selected by `--checksums-file`, explicit configuration, or the codec default. For a versioned embedded filename, pass that filename explicitly. With a public key, signatures are checked before decoding; the selected signature format must exist (WordPress `.sig` by default; pass `--signature-format minisign` or `ssh` for those formats), and all other present supported signature formats are also authenticated when verifying the embedded manifest. Without a public key, checksums detect changes but do not authenticate the publisher. Trusted public keys must come from outside the package."],[347,"Verify"],[350,"Version check"],[190,"When `svn status` is empty, the deploy ends without a commit. A dry run logs the status and stops before the commit. Otherwise the changes are committed, and the temporary directory is always removed."],[56,"When the changesets of a pull request release a source, `changeset-status` requires no changeset for a dependent whose only change is the spec of that source in its `package.json`, since versioning rewrites the spec and the bundle note announces it. Any other change to the dependent, like another manifest field or dependency, documentation or code, needs its own changeset."],[205,"When the files are the same as in the previous release, that release is reused. Otherwise a release commit is created with the files, whose parents are the previous release and the source history: the source commit (`--source-ref`, default `HEAD`), or with `--split-prefix` the history of that directory from `git subtree split`. The repository keeps the source history, and `git log --first-parent` lists the releases."],[206,"When the previous release on `--branch` already has newer source history, the branch isn't moved, so running an older build again, like a re-run of an earlier CI job, can't release older files. The source history is outdated when it's an older commit of the source history that the previous release was made from, its last parent. `git subtree split` makes this reliable for `--split-prefix` too, since it creates the same commits for the same history. A revert is a new commit, so it's released, and with `--tag` the tag of an older build is still released, without moving the branch."],[186,"With `--trunk` or `--svn-tag`, the files must be a plugin whose `Version` header matches the version. When `--trunk` and `--svn-tag` are combined, the readme `Stable tag` must match too; a tag-only run skips that, since a pre-release tag doesn't change the stable tag. `--readme-only` needs the readme. Nothing is checked out when a check fails."],[98,"With the `github` log format, the CLI uses current GitHub Actions features to report problems where you look for them, instead of only in the log. `--log-format <name>` picks how the CLI logs, separately from the result that `--json` prints:"],[48,"Without `--since`, `changeset-status` compares against `<remote>/<baseBranch>`, where `remote` in the config defaults to `origin` and is also the remote that `changeset-tag --push` pushes tags to; fetch that ref or pass `--since` when using a fork or different upstream. `loadChangesetConfig()` discovers a conventional config at the repository root, unless `--config <path>` selects one explicitly."],[50,"Without a terminal, or for scripts, pass the changeset as options: `npx wp-release changeset-add --package @vendor/plugin --bump patch --summary \"Fix the checkout validation.\"`. Repeat package and bump flags for multiple packages, or use `--empty` when no release is needed. A package can also be named by its path under `root`, like `plugins/alpha`, and the changeset records its identifier; `changeset-version --package` and `changeset-tag --package` accept paths too. A single `--bump` without `--package` releases every changed package without a changeset, like `changeset-add --bump patch --summary \"…\"` to fix a failing `changeset-status` check. `--json` and `--github-output` never ask."],[293,"WordPress internationalization"],[298,"WordPress internationalization"],[285,"WordPress.org themes are released by uploading a ZIP, so `deploy-svn` is for plugins only. Use `zip` to build a theme upload. Packaging and deployment options follow familiar community tools to ease migration; the comparison explains the differences."],[278,"WP Release"],[237,"wp-release has two layers, so that it fits any repository layout:"],[385,"Write the manifest into the directory that is zipped and deployed. The `checksums` command leaves its output manifest and its signatures (`<name>.sig`, `<name>.minisig` and `<name>.sshsig`) out of the hashes, so running it again with the same output path and `--force` gives the same manifest. `verify` checks matching copies as described below. Sign it last, since the signature covers the exact bytes of the manifest."],[340,"ZIP"],[333,"ZIP files and metadata:"]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":19,"2":20,"3":21,"4":22,"5":23,"6":24,"7":25,"8":26,"9":27,"10":28,"11":29,"12":30,"13":31,"14":32,"15":33,"16":34,"17":35,"18":36,"19":37,"20":38,"21":39,"22":40,"23":41,"24":42,"25":43,"26":44,"27":45,"28":46,"29":47,"30":48,"31":49,"32